__kbPart({"name":"ROKAE","content":{"ROKAE-Developer-Guide":"---\ntype: dev-guide\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, dev-guide]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics — Developer Guide (hackathon edition)\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](ROKAE.md) · [Product portfolio](ROKAE-Products.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](ROKAE-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> Research note: built from ROKAE's official docs portal and official GitHub account (fetched 2026-11-14). Open web search was not available for this pass, so community / third-party assets (MuJoCo, Isaac, LeRobot ports) could not be checked — they are marked accordingly.\n\n## TL;DR — how you program it\n\n- **One SDK for every arm: xCore SDK** (C++, Python, C#, Android/Java), Apache-2.0, on GitHub under `RokaeRobot`. It talks to the xCore controller over **TCP/IP**; the real-time channel uses **UDP**.\n- **Python = high-level only.** Point-to-point and linear moves, jog, drag teaching, force-control setup, I/O, registers. Enough for most 48-hour builds (pick-and-place, compliant contact, massage-style demos).\n- **C++ = everything, including 1 kHz real-time mode** with five controller modes on xMate cobots: joint position, Cartesian position, joint impedance, Cartesian impedance, **direct torque**. Needs a wired direct link and ideally a PREEMPT_RT Linux PC.\n- **ROS 2 Humble package (`rokae_ros2`)** with `ros2_control`, MoveIt 2 configs per model, URDF/xacro and Gazebo — the fastest way to get planning plus simulation without touching the SDK.\n- **No downloadable AI models or datasets.** ROKAE supplies force-controlled hardware and control software; bring your own policy and perception, plus an external PC (the arm controller has no GPU).\n\n## SDKs and interfaces\n\n| SDK / repo | Language | What it controls | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| xCoreSDK-CPP | C++ (CMake ≥ 3.12) | All xCore robots: xMate cobots, AR 7-DoF arms, NB/XB industrial arms. Non-real-time motion, real-time mode, PC-side planner, kinematics/dynamics model (`xMateModel`) | [GitHub](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP) | Ubuntu 18.04 / 20.04 / 22.04 (x86_64, aarch64), Windows 10/11 (MSVC 14.1+). Repo has headers + examples; **prebuilt libs come from the Releases page** and go in `lib/`. README references **v0.7.1**, controller **xCore ≥ 3.2.1** |\n| xCoreSDK-Python | Python **3.8–3.12** | Same robots, **non-real-time only**: motion queue, jog, drag, force control, I/O, registers, RL projects | [GitHub](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python) | **Not on pip.** Clone, then extract the matching release archive (win / linux-x86_64 / linux-aarch64) into `Release/`. First script: `example/base_example.py` |\n| xCoreSDK-CSharp | C# | Same scope as Python | [GitHub](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CSharp) | Windows only |\n| xCoreSDK-Android | Java | Same scope as Python | [GitHub account](https://github.com/RokaeRobot) | Last updated 2023 — treat as stale |\n| rokae_ros2 | C++ / ROS 2 Humble | xMate SR / CR / ER / Pro, AR arms, several NB/XB arms | [GitHub](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2) · [manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2) | `ros2_control` hardware interface, MoveIt 2, Gazebo, driver services (movej / movel / IK / DI-DO / drag) |\n| xCorePlugin-client / -controller | C++ | Extensions to the pendant software and controller | [GitHub account](https://github.com/RokaeRobot) | Not needed for a hackathon build |\n| Arc-welding SDK | C++ / Python / C# | Welding process package | [SDK docs](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK) | Only relevant with the welding robot |\n| Helios \"ROS 2 + Python SDK\" | — | Wheeled dual-arm robot | [Helios page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html) | Listed on the product page; **no public repo or docs found** — ask ROKAE staff on site |\n| iNex | — | Whole-body controller for wheeled humanoids / dual-arm systems | not public | 🧪 Described in ROKAE news only; no public API |\n\nDoc versions on the portal: **0.7.x**, **0.7.x-AR** (humanoid arm) and **0.8.x**. C++ changelog: v0.7.0 (2026.1), v0.7.1 (2026.3), v0.7.2 (2026.6, for xCore 3.2.2). **Match SDK version to the controller firmware on the robot you are given.**\n\nRobot classes (pick by model; the SDK checks the class against the connected robot):\n\n| Class | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `xMateRobot` | 6-axis cobots (SR, CR, …) |\n| `xMateErProRobot` | 7-axis arms — ER Pro **and AR series** |\n| `xMateCr5Robot` | CR5 (5-axis) |\n| `StandardRobot` | 6-axis industrial NB / XB |\n\n## Control levels\n\n| Level | Interface | Rate | What you get | Requirements |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pendant / no-code | RobotAssist HMI, drag teaching, RL programs | — | Teach points, run programs, calibrate tool/load and force sensor | None |\n| High-level (non-real-time) | Python / C# / C++ SDK, ROS 2 driver services | Command queue, not cyclic | `MoveAbsJ`, `MoveJ`, `MoveL`, `MoveC`, `MoveCF`, `MoveSP`; jog; drag and path replay (xMate only); collision detection; force-control setup (`fcInit` / `fcStart`, Cartesian or joint stiffness, desired force); DI/DO, registers, RS-485 | Any OS above; switch or direct cable both work |\n| Trajectory streaming | ROS 2 `ros2_control` + MoveIt 2 | Real-robot controllers default to **1000 Hz** update | `follow_joint_trajectory`, MoveIt planning, RViz | Ubuntu 22.04 + Humble; RT kernel recommended |\n| Low-level real-time | **C++ only** (`RtMotionControl`) | **up to 1 kHz** | xMate cobots: joint position, Cartesian position, joint impedance, Cartesian impedance, **direct torque**. 6-axis industrial arms: joint + Cartesian position only | Direct cable to the controller's control LAN port, no switch; whole loop (network round trip + your code + robot) **under 1 ms**; timeout configurable 1–4 ms in `xcoresdk_config.json` |\n\n- **EDU / developer editions:** the docs state no separate licence or paid option for real-time mode. Whether the on-site units have it enabled — ⚠️ unverified, ask staff.\n- **AR arms in real-time mode:** the AR FAQ documents **joint position and Cartesian position** demos. Impedance / torque modes on AR are not documented there — ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Published torque-mode limits** (docs list only these models):\n\n| Model | Axes | Max torque | Max torque rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| xMate ER3 Pro / ER7 Pro | 1–4 | **85 Nm** | 1500 Nm/s |\n| | 5–7 | **36 Nm** | 1000 Nm/s |\n| xMate3 / xMate7 | 1–3 | **85 Nm** | 1500 Nm/s |\n| | 4–6 | **36 Nm** | 1000 Nm/s |\n\nRecommendation for 48 hours: **stay in Python non-real-time + built-in force control** unless your idea really needs a custom control law. Real-time mode costs half a day of RT-kernel and network tuning.\n\n## ROS 2 and middleware\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Package | `rokae_ros2` v0.1.1 — [repo](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2) |\n| Supported stack | **ROS 2 Humble on Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64** only (other distros \"may be incompatible\") |\n| Sub-packages | `rokae_hardware` (ros2_control SystemInterface + drivers), `rokae_description` (URDF/xacro, meshes), `rokae_msgs`, `rokae_<model>_moveit_config`, `rokae_gazebo` |\n| `robot_type` values | SR3 / SR4 / SR5 · CR7 / CR12 / CR18 / CR20 / CR35 · ER3 / ER7 · Pro3 / Pro7 · AR3L / AR3R / AR5L / AR5R (+ variants) · XB / NB industrial models |\n| Dual-arm wheeled platform | **RViz visualisation only** — no control, MoveIt or Gazebo |\n| Topics | `/joint_states`, `/rokae_driver/joint_states`, `/rokae_driver/cartesian_pose` |\n| Driver services (`/rokae_driver/…`, 7-axis: `/rokae_driver7/…`) | `movej`, `movel`, `movec`, `jog_control`, `drag_control`, `calculate_fk`, `calculate_ik`, `get/set_di`, `get/set_do`, `read/write_register`, `get_robot_info` |\n| Actions | `/move_action`, `/execute_trajectory`, `/position_joint_trajectory_controller/follow_joint_trajectory` |\n\nLaunch lines from the manual:\n\n```bash\n# MoveIt with fake hardware (no robot needed)\nros2 launch rokae_hardware rokae_moveit_launch.py robot_type:=SR4 use_fake_hardware:=true\n# MoveIt on the real arm\nros2 launch rokae_hardware rokae_moveit_launch.py robot_type:=SR5 use_fake_hardware:=false robot_ip:=<robot> local_ip:=<your NIC>\n# Driver only (services + state topics)\nros2 launch rokae_hardware rokae_driver.launch.py robot_ip:=<robot> local_ip:=<your NIC>\n```\n\nNo force/torque or impedance topics are listed in the ROS 2 manual — for contact tasks call the SDK directly or wrap it in your own node.\n\n## Simulation and digital twins\n\n| Asset | Status | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| URDF / xacro + meshes | ✅ official | `rokae_description` in [rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2) |\n| Gazebo (via `gazebo_ros2_control`) | ✅ official | `rokae_gazebo`: `empty.world`, `obstacles.world`, record / replay scripts, unified `gazebo_moveit.launch.py mode:=sim|real` |\n| MoveIt 2 fake hardware | ✅ official | `use_fake_hardware:=true` |\n| MuJoCo MJCF | not found in official repos | Convert the URDF yourself; community models ⚠️ unverified |\n| Isaac Sim / Isaac Lab USD | not found in official repos | Import the URDF; ⚠️ unverified |\n| RokaeStudio offline programming | ✅ vendor tool | [Download center](https://www.rokae.com/en/service/190/Download-Center.html) (some downloads need a MyROKAE login) |\n| Helios / Human.X simulation assets | not public | — |\n\n## AI stack\n\n| Topic | What exists publicly |\n|---|---|\n| Pretrained policies / VLA | **None downloadable.** WRC 2026 demos used a self-developed \"embodied skill model\" with imitation learning + human-in-the-loop RL (USB insertion, reported 95–99 % success) — not released |\n| Foundation-model interface | iNex is described as exposing interfaces for teleoperation devices and model training 🧪 — no public docs |\n| Datasets | not public |\n| Teleoperation / data collection | Shown with third-party kit: IO-AI TeleXperience, **Wuji Hand**, motion capture, exoskeleton gloves. No ROKAE-published pipeline. Helios lists gamepad control only |\n| LeRobot support | No official integration found. Practical route: write a small LeRobot robot class around the Python SDK (`jointPos()` for state, `MoveAbsJ` queue for actions) — expect low action rates in non-real-time mode |\n| Built-in \"skills\" worth using | Drag teaching + path replay, Cartesian / joint impedance, desired-force control, collision detection with selectable reaction (stop or compliant) |\n\nFastest learning pipeline for a weekend: kinesthetic demos by **drag teaching** (`enableDrag`), log `jointPos()` and camera frames on your PC, train a small imitation policy, execute through the motion queue. **WUJI is on site** — the same hand ROKAE used in its own teleoperation demo.\n\n## Compute, sensors and connectivity\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Arm controller | xCore controller (compact MSC / MAC units for SR / AR). No GPU — runs motion control only |\n| Your PC | Linux x86_64 or aarch64 (Jetson and Raspberry Pi 3B+/4/5 work if glibc ≥ 2.27), or Windows 10/11 |\n| Default network | Direct-connect port: static **192.168.0.160**. Set your PC to the same subnet, e.g. 192.168.0.22. A second port is for router / switch networks (docs warn this can make motion unstable) |\n| Other IPs seen in docs | ROS 2 manual examples use 192.168.2.160 (SR5) and 192.168.21.10 (industrial) — **read the actual IP from the pendant** |\n| Protocol | TCP for commands, UDP for real-time state/commands; `connectToRobot(remoteIP, localIP)` — the local IP is needed for the real-time/state stream |\n| Built-in sensing | Joint torque sensors on xMate / AR: `jointTorque()`, external force via `getEndTorque(...)`; DI/DO; RS-485 on some controllers |\n| Not included | Camera, gripper, GPU — plan your own (D-Robotics RDK board or a laptop for perception) |\n| Helios (if on site) | Compute option: Orin NX 8 GB, AGX Orin 64 GB or Thor T5000. 2× 3D LiDAR, head stereo camera, IMU; optional fisheye / chest / wrist cameras, mic array. Wi-Fi 2.4/5 GHz, Bluetooth, optional 5G; HDMI, USB 2.0, RJ45 debug ports. ~4 h runtime |\n\n## First 60 minutes checklist\n\n1. **Ask ROKAE staff (5 min):** exact model, controller firmware version (xCore 3.x), robot IP, which SDK version they recommend, whether real-time mode is allowed, where the e-stop is.\n2. **Safety setup:** clear the workspace, e-stop within reach, confirm tool and **payload mass / centre of mass are entered on the pendant** (wrong load data makes force control drift).\n3. **Cable:** PC straight into the robot's direct-connect port. Set a static IP on the same subnet (default robot IP 192.168.0.160). `ping` it.\n4. **Get the SDK:** `git clone https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python`, download the matching release archive from the repo's Releases page, extract into `Release/`. Python 3.8–3.12.\n5. **Verify:** edit the IP in `example/base_example.py` and run it from `example/`. It should connect and print robot info / state.\n6. **Hello-world motion (slow speed first).** Documented call order — take exact enum names and argument shapes from `base_example.py`:\n   - create the right class (`xMateRobot` for 6-axis, `xMateErProRobot` for 7-axis/AR) and `connectToRobot(...)`\n   - `setOperateMode(` automatic `)` → `setPowerState(` on `)`\n   - `setMotionControlMode(` NrtCommandMode `)`\n   - `moveReset()` → `moveAppend(` MoveAbsJ / MoveL command(s) `)` → `moveStart()`\n   - `stop()` to halt; power off when done\n7. **Read sensors:** `jointPos()`, `posture(...)`, `jointTorque()`, `getEndTorque(...)`. Push gently on the flange and watch the values change.\n8. **Try compliance:** `enableDrag(...)` and move the arm by hand, then `disableDrag()`. For contact tasks: `fcInit` → `setControlType` → `setCartesianStiffness` / `setCartesianDesiredForce` → `fcStart` … `fcStop`.\n9. **Enable collision detection** before any demo near people: `enableCollisionDetection(sensitivity, behaviour, fallback)`.\n10. **ROS 2 route instead (optional):** on Ubuntu 22.04 + Humble, build `rokae_ros2`, run the fake-hardware MoveIt launch first, then the real launch with `robot_ip` and `local_ip`.\n\n## Known gotchas and safety\n\n| Gotcha | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| Python has **no real-time mode**, no PC-side planner, no `xMateModel` | Use C++ for 1 kHz loops or dynamics |\n| SDK not installable via pip; repo ships without binaries | Download the release archive that matches OS, CPU and Python version |\n| SDK / firmware mismatch | README requires xCore ≥ 3.2.1 for v0.7.1; AR FAQ still references an older AR-specific pair (controller 3.0.1.6.AR…, SDK v0.5.1.ar) — confirm versions with staff |\n| Real-time mode over a switch or Wi-Fi | Use a direct cable; docs recommend a **Realtek NIC over Intel**, `performance` CPU governor, PREEMPT_RT kernel |\n| 1 kHz callback overruns | No printing, file I/O, sleeping, sockets or allocation in the loop; raise the timeout (1–4 ms) or network tolerance if packets drop |\n| After switching to real-time mode the robot stays powered and comes up in automatic mode after restart | Be aware before rebooting next to people |\n| Torque mode: sag, float or drift | Almost always wrong payload / COM or an uncalibrated force sensor. Calibrate (pendant or `calibrateForceSensor`), set `setGravityDirection` for side mounts. Controller adds gravity + friction compensation to your commanded torque |\n| Torque jumps at start | Ramp from small values; respect torque and torque-rate limits |\n| Noisy `getEndTorque` | Compute external torque from `tau_m` minus model gravity (and Coriolis when moving) |\n| State values all zero or lagging in C++ | Request the field in `startReceiveRobotState`, use the same period in `updateRobotState`, flush stale packets first |\n| AR arms | Use the 7-axis class; shipped Cartesian demo is written for 6-axis and will not run unchanged; left and right side-mount parameters differ; set automatic + real-time mode **before** power-on; call `setDefaultConfOpt(false)` if IK/FK fails |\n| ROS 2 | Humble / 22.04 only; 6-axis vs 7-axis SDK type is switched manually in `rokae_hardware_interface`; check the pendant's RCI setting and packet loss on the real robot; test with fake hardware first |\n| Helios DoF | Product page says 42 in the text and 23 in the spec table (news says 43) — confirm before planning whole-body tasks |\n| Human contact (T04) | Collision detection and impedance reduce risk but these are **not certified medical devices**. Low stiffness, low speed, e-stop in the operator's hand, test on a dummy first |\n\n## Community resources\n\n| Resource | Link | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Docs portal (SDK, xCore manual, ROS 2, plugins, downloads) | [docs.rokae.com/en](https://docs.rokae.com/en/) | English pages exist; some sub-page URLs and comments are Chinese |\n| C++ real-time troubleshooting (network, torque, AR) | [torque control FAQ](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/cpp/%E5%AE%9E%E6%97%B6%E6%A8%A1%E5%BC%8F%E5%B8%B8%E8%A7%81%E9%97%AE%E9%A2%98/cpp_rt_torque_control) · [AR FAQ](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/cpp/%E5%AE%9E%E6%97%B6%E6%A8%A1%E5%BC%8F%E5%B8%B8%E8%A7%81%E9%97%AE%E9%A2%98/cpp_rt_ar_special) | Best source of practical gotchas |\n| GitHub issues | [RokaeRobot](https://github.com/RokaeRobot) | Small community: xCoreSDK-CPP ~46 stars, a handful of open issues |\n| Forum / Discord | not found | The on-site ROKAE engineers are the realistic support channel |\n\n## Sources\n\n- [ROKAE documentation hub](https://docs.rokae.com/en/)\n- [xCore SDK landing page](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [SDK introduction (platforms, real-time modes, language support)](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/SDK_Intro)\n- [SDK quick start (network, build, RT kernel)](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/quick_start)\n- [SDK FAQ](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/sdk_faq)\n- [C++ SDK introduction (version history)](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/cpp/cpp_Intro)\n- [Python SDK introduction](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/python/py_Intro)\n- [Python SDK API description](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/python/py_API)\n- [C++ real-time mode — torque control issues](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/cpp/%E5%AE%9E%E6%97%B6%E6%A8%A1%E5%BC%8F%E5%B8%B8%E8%A7%81%E9%97%AE%E9%A2%98/cpp_rt_torque_control)\n- [C++ real-time mode — AR models](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/cpp/%E5%AE%9E%E6%97%B6%E6%A8%A1%E5%BC%8F%E5%B8%B8%E8%A7%81%E9%97%AE%E9%A2%98/cpp_rt_ar_special)\n- [ROS 2 package manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2)\n- [Rokae xMate ROS 2 user manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2/rokae%20ros2%E4%BD%BF%E7%94%A8%E6%89%8B%E5%86%8C)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot account](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [GitHub — xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — xCoreSDK-Python](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python)\n- [GitHub — rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE news — WRC 2026 full-stack development platform](https://www.rokae.com/en/news/show/2604/WRC-2026:-ROKAE-Unveils-a-Ready-to-Use-Full-Stack-Development-Platform-for-Embodied-Intelligence,-Advancing-Physical-AI-Infrastructure-Development.html)\n- [ROKAE download center](https://www.rokae.com/en/service/190/Download-Center.html)\n","ROKAE-Products":"---\ntype: products\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, products]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics — Product Portfolio\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](ROKAE.md) · [Developer guide](ROKAE-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](ROKAE-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> **Read this first:** every ROKAE cobot and AR arm is a **force-controlled** arm (flange force/torque sensing, adjustable Cartesian stiffness). Pick ROKAE when your build *touches* something — a person, a soft object, an unknown surface. What ROKAE actually brought to Leukerbad is **not confirmed**; the most plausible units are a small SR / CR cobot or AR arms. Prices are **not public** for any product (quote only).\n\n## Lineup map\n\nTrack relevance: ● strong · ◐ partial · ○ none. \"Launched\" is given only where a source supports it.\n\n| Product | Category | Status | Launched | Price | Positioning (one line) | T01 | T02 | T03 | T04 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| xMate SR series (SR3, SR5) | Compact 6-axis force-controlled cobot | ✅ | not confirmed (page assets date from 2022) | not public | Lightest ROKAE cobot, 48 V, 1.2 kg controller — the easy bench arm for contact demos | ◐ | ○ | ◐ | ● |\n| xMate CR series (CR5 … CR35) | 6-axis (two 5-axis) cobot | ✅ | not confirmed | not public | Workhorse cobot family, **5–45 kg**, mostly IP67, TÜV-certified safety controller | ◐ | ◐ | ● | ◐ |\n| xMate ZR series (ZR6) | 6-axis cobot | ✅ | not confirmed (page assets dated Oct 2026) | not public | New-generation cobot built on HSA joints, controller built into the arm | ◐ | ○ | ◐ | ◐ |\n| xMate ER Pro (ER3 Pro / ER7 Pro) | 7-DoF cobot with joint torque sensors | ⚠️ unverified (legacy) | not confirmed | not public | Older 7-axis research / medical arm; no longer on the English product pages | ○ | ○ | ○ | ◐ |\n| AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10) | 7-DoF humanoid force-controlled arm | ✅ | not confirmed (10,000th unit delivered Oct 2026) | not public | Light human-like arm for humanoids, dual-arm rigs and data collection | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ● |\n| HSA series (HSA-11 … HSA-32) | Integrated force-controlled joint module | ✅ | not confirmed | not public | Build-your-own-robot actuator with torque sensor, EtherCAT | ○ | ○ | ○ | ◐ |\n| Helios (Helios II) | Wheeled dual-arm humanoid | ✅ | not confirmed (data sheet dated Sep 2026) | not public | Only ROKAE platform with mobility + two arms + on-board GPU | ● | ◐ | ◐ | ● |\n| Human.X | Full-size bipedal humanoid | 🧪 | ~mid-2026 (⚠️ unverified, single secondary source) | not public | Biped showcase; developer access not public | ◐ | ◐ | ○ | ◐ |\n| CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR) | Mobile manipulator (AMR + cobot) | ✅ | not confirmed | not public | Factory AMR carrying an SR / CR arm; heavy (150–1500 kg chassis) | ◐ | ○ | ◐ | ○ |\n| Intelligent Welding Robot series | Cobot welding package | ✅ | not confirmed | not public | SR / CR arm + welding kit for small-batch welding | ○ | ○ | ○ | ○ |\n| NB / XB series | 6-axis industrial robots | ✅ | not confirmed | not public | Fenced industrial arms, **4–220 kg** | ○ | ○ | ◐ | ○ |\n| xCore / iNex | Control system / embodied-AI control system | ✅ xCore · 🧪 iNex | not confirmed | bundled | Controller software behind every arm; SDK entry point | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ |\n\n## Products\n\n### xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)\n\n![xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)](assets/rokae-xmate-sr.jpg)\n\nCompact 6-axis force-controlled cobot with the controller moved out of the base into a small separate box, so the arm fits tight mounting spots. **Most likely ROKAE arm on site** (best guess, not confirmed).\n\n| Spec | SR3-3/0.7C | SR5-5/0.9C |\n|---|---|---|\n| DoF | 6 | 6 |\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** |\n| Reach | **705 mm** | **919 mm** |\n| Arm weight | about 13.8 kg | about 16.5 kg |\n| Repeatability | ±0.03 mm | ±0.03 mm |\n| Max tool speed | ≤1.5 m/s | ≤2.0 m/s |\n| Joint speed | 180°/s all axes | 180°/s all axes |\n| Force sensing (tool flange) | Force x-y-z, torque x-y-z | Force x-y-z, torque x-y-z |\n| Relative force-control accuracy (as listed) | 0.5 N / 0.1 Nm | 0.1 N / 0.02 Nm |\n| Cartesian stiffness range | 0–3000 N/m, 0–300 Nm/rad | 0–3000 N/m, 0–300 Nm/rad |\n| Power | 48 VDC | 48 VDC |\n| IP rating (arm) | IP54 | IP54 |\n| Mounting | Any angle | Any angle |\n| Operating temperature | 0–50 °C | 0–50 °C |\n\n**Controller — xMate SR Cab (MSC):** 203 × 175 × 42 mm, about **1.2 kg**, IP20, 48 VDC input · 5 DI / 5 DO · safety IO (1 safety gate, 1 external e-stop, 1 safety output) · RS232 ×1, RS485 ×1, USB 3.0 ×2, EtherCAT ×1, Gigabit Ethernet ×3 · optional digital / analog IO and encoder modules.\n\n- **Variants:** two models only. No separate EDU / developer edition is listed — SDK access is the same xCore SDK for all cobots (see [Developer guide](ROKAE-Developer-Guide.md)).\n- **Typical applications (official cases):** AI massage robot, robotic coffee and ice cream, tomato and grape picking, chemical catalyst preparation, small-part loading, ship-deck welding.\n- **Hackathon notes:** 48 V DC supply and a 1.2 kg controller make it portable; the arm is IP54 and the controller IP20 — keep it indoors and dry. No gripper, camera or GPU included.\n- Official page: [SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n\n### xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)\n\n![xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)](assets/rokae-xmate-cr.jpg)\n\nROKAE's main cobot family: force-position hybrid control on the xCore controller, a separate control cabinet with more IO, and a built-in TÜV-certified safety controller (ISO 13849-1, up to PL d). Nine models.\n\n| Model | DoF | Payload | Reach | Arm weight | Repeatability | Max tool speed | IP (arm) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CR5-5/1.4C | 6 | **5 kg** | 1406 mm | ~36 kg | ±0.03 mm | ≤3.0 m/s | IP54 |\n| CR7-7/0.98C | 6 | **7 kg** | 988 mm | ~25 kg | ±0.02 mm | ≤3.2 m/s | IP67 |\n| CR12-12/1.4C | 6 | **12 kg** | 1434 mm | ~41 kg | ±0.03 mm | ≤3.0 m/s | IP67 |\n| CR18-18/1.0C | 6 | **18 kg** | 1062 mm | ~38 kg | ±0.03 mm | ≤3.0 m/s | IP67 |\n| CR20-20/1.8C | 6 | **20 kg** | 1798 mm | ~71 kg | ±0.05 mm | ≤3.5 m/s | IP67 |\n| CR20-25/1.8C-5 | 5 | **25 kg** | 1798 mm | ~69 kg | ±0.05 mm | ≤3.5 m/s | IP67 |\n| CR20-17/2.0C-5 | 5 | **17 kg** | 2047 mm | ~71 kg | ±0.05 mm | ≤4.0 m/s | IP67 |\n| CR35-35/2.2C | 6 | **35 kg** | 2246 mm | ~180 kg | ±0.05 mm | not checked | IP67 |\n| CR35-45/1.9C | 6 | **45 kg** | 1947 mm (per CMR-L table) | not checked | ±0.05 mm | not checked | not checked |\n\nCommon to the models below 35 kg (checked on CR5 / CR7): flange force x-y-z and torque x-y-z sensing · Cartesian stiffness **0–6000 N/m**, 0–1000 Nm/rad · relative force-control accuracy 0.5 N / 0.1 Nm · 48 VDC arm supply · any-angle mounting · 0–50 °C.\n\n| Controller | xMate Control Cab (MCC) | xMate Control Cab Mix (MCCM) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Used with | CR below 35 kg, ZR | CR 35 kg and above |\n| Size / weight | 450 × 250 × 350 mm · ~16.6 kg | 480 × 325 × 360 mm · ~28.4 kg |\n| Input power | Single-phase 90–264 V AC (180–264 V AC for CR20) | Single-phase 190–240 V AC |\n| IP | IP54 | IP54 |\n| IO | 16 DI / 16 DO, 5 safety inputs, 4 safety outputs (dual channel) | same |\n| Comms | Gigabit Ethernet ×2, USB ×2, EtherCAT ×1, RS232 or RS485 ×1 | RS232 ×1, Ethernet ×4, USB 3.0 ×1, EtherCAT ×1 |\n\n- **Variants:** the \"-5\" models are 5-axis long-reach palletizing versions. No EDU edition listed.\n- **Typical applications (official cases):** palletizing (food, medical supplies), SMT reel loading, warehouse picking and sorting (CR35), battery cell handling, AI / 3D vision inspection, sand-core finishing.\n- **Hackathon notes:** CR7 (25 kg, IP67 arm) and CR12 are the realistic portable sizes; they need the 16.6 kg MCC cabinet and mains power. Plugs into Swiss 230 V without issue (90–264 V AC input).\n- Official page: [xMate CR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n\n### xMate ZR series (ZR6)\n\n![xMate ZR series (ZR6)](assets/rokae-xmate-zr.jpg)\n\nNew-generation full-joint force-controlled cobot built on ROKAE's HSA joint modules; shares the xCore platform and software ecosystem with CR / SR. One model so far.\n\n| Spec | ZR6-6/0.98C |\n|---|---|\n| DoF | 6 |\n| Payload | **6 kg** |\n| Reach | **980 mm** |\n| Weight | about **20 kg** (with built-in control cabinet) |\n| Repeatability | ±0.05 mm |\n| Max tool speed | ≤3.0 m/s |\n| Force sensing | Flange force x-y-z, torque x-y-z; relative accuracy 0.5 N / 0.1 Nm |\n| Cartesian stiffness range | 0–3000 N/m, 0–300 Nm/rad |\n| Power | 48 VDC |\n| IP rating | IP54 |\n| Mounting | Any angle |\n\n- ⚠️ The page lists the weight \"with built-in control cabinet\" but also shows the generic MCC cabinet table — confirm with the vendor which controller ships.\n- Typical applications: same industrial segments as CR (auto parts, electronics, general industry); no specific case studies published yet.\n- Official page: [xMate ZR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/626/xMate-ZR.html)\n\n### xMate ER Pro (ER3 Pro / ER7 Pro)\n\nNo picture saved (no current official product page).\n\nOlder 7-DoF cobot with joint torque sensors. **Status ⚠️ unverified:** not in the current English product navigation (only CR / SR / ZR are listed); still appears in ROKAE Japan case studies, for example a teleoperated ultrasound robot built on ER7 Pro.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| DoF | 7 |\n| Payload (ER7 Pro) | **7 kg** (per profile research) |\n| Reach (ER7 Pro) | **850 mm** (per profile research) |\n| Other specs | not checked — no current official spec page |\n\n- If you need a 7-DoF ROKAE arm today, the **AR series** is the current product.\n\n### AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)\n\n![AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)](assets/rokae-ar.jpg)\n\n7-DoF human-like arm with a cross-wrist layout and joint torque sensors as standard. ROKAE's 2026 headline product, sold both as a stand-alone arm and as the arm of Helios and of other makers' humanoids. Aimed explicitly at AI, data collection and training.\n\n| Spec | AR3 | AR5 | AR10 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DoF | 7 | 7 | 7 |\n| Rated payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** | **10 kg** |\n| Operating radius | **650 mm** | **683 mm** | **815 mm** |\n| Arm weight | **8 kg** | **10 kg** | **19.5 kg** |\n| Repeatability | ±0.05 mm | ±0.05 mm | ±0.1 mm |\n| Absolute accuracy | ±1 mm | ±1 mm | ±1 mm |\n| Max TCP speed | ≥2 m/s | ≥2 m/s | ≥2 m/s |\n| Joint hollow bore | 6 mm | 9 mm | 10 mm |\n| IP rating | IP54 | IP54 | IP54 |\n| Brakes | On joints | On joints | On joints |\n\nCommon to all three: joint torque sensors standard · force-control accuracy ≤0.15 N / ≤0.05 Nm and resolution ≤0.05 N / ≤0.02 Nm (reference values) · force sensor sampling ≥5 kHz, overload ≥300% F.S. · torque loop closed in the controller (basic scheme) or in the joint (advanced scheme) · automatic payload identification · **48 V DC (±15%)** · **EtherCAT, 1 ms cycle** · noise ≤60 dB · 0–50 °C.\n\n- **Variants:** three payload sizes. Left / right or dual-arm kits and an EDU edition are not listed publicly. The docs portal has a dedicated SDK branch (0.7.x-AR) — see [Developer guide](ROKAE-Developer-Guide.md).\n- **End effectors:** \"multiple end-effector interfaces\" claimed; specific supported grippers / hands not listed on the page.\n- **Typical applications:** humanoid and wheeled dual-arm robots, AI data collection and training, smart warehousing, factory automation.\n- **Hackathon notes:** 8–10 kg arm weight means an AR3 / AR5 could ride on another vendor's mobile base, but integration is EtherCAT-level work — only attempt if ROKAE staff supply the controller and mount.\n- Official page: [Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n\n### HSA series (HSA-11 … HSA-32)\n\n![HSA series (HSA-11 … HSA-32)](assets/rokae-hsa.jpg)\n\nIntegrated joint module: frameless torque motor, gear, brake option, dual absolute encoders and a torque sensor in one hollow-shaft unit. The building block of the AR arms and the ZR cobot.\n\n| Spec | HSA-11 | HSA-14 | HSA-17 | HSA-20 | HSA-25 | HSA-32 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Diameter / length (mm) | 52 / 68 | 60 / 74 | 72 / 98 | 82 / 107 | 110 / 115 | 142 / 135 |\n| Hollow bore (mm) | 6 | 9 | 10 | 13 | 15 | 21 |\n| Weight (g) | **450** (no brake) | 600 | 1100 | 1700 | 2950 | **6000** |\n| Rated power (W) | 40 | 120 | 160 | 250 | 600 | 950 |\n| Gear ratio | 100 | 100 | 100 | 120 | 100 | 120 |\n| Rated speed (rpm) | 30 | 30 | 30 | 30 | 30 | 20 |\n| Peak torque (Nm) | **11** | 19 | 70 | 113 | 204 | **459** |\n| Torque sensor rated torque (Nm) | 12 | 25 | 70 | 110 | 210 | 480 |\n\nCommon: 48 V · torque sensor resolution ≤0.05 Nm · force-control bandwidth 100 Hz · impedance control at **1 kHz** · force sampling 32 kHz · 18-bit dual absolute encoders · **EtherCAT**.\n\n- **Hackathon notes:** a component, not a robot. Useful only if a team is building a custom mechanism and ROKAE supplies drivers and an EtherCAT master setup — not a 48-hour starting point.\n- Official page: [Integrated Force-Controlled Joint HSA](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/598/Integrated-Force-Controlled-Joint-HSA.html)\n\n### Helios (Helios II)\n\n![Helios (Helios II)](assets/rokae-helios.jpg)\n\nWheeled dual-arm humanoid: omnidirectional base, lifting torso, two 7-DoF force-controlled arms, LiDAR SLAM navigation. The only ROKAE product that is a complete mobile robot with on-board AI compute. Five units run at Schaeffler's Taicang plant.\n\n| Spec | Helios II – 5 |\n|---|---|\n| Size (L×W×H) | 750 × 650 × **1493–2000 mm** |\n| Weight | **160 kg** |\n| DoF | **23** in the spec table (neck 2, arms 7×2, waist 2, legs 2, chassis 3). The intro text says 42 — presumably counting hands; ⚠️ unverified |\n| Payload per arm | **5 kg** |\n| Force control | Joint force sensors; whole-body force control for arms, waist and neck |\n| End effectors (optional) | Electric grippers, dexterous hands, electric vacuum grippers |\n| Chassis | Omnidirectional, 4 drive wheels, **1.5 m/s** max, docking ±10 mm |\n| Perception | 2× 3D navigation LiDAR · head stereo camera · IMU · optional: 3 fisheye cameras, chest stereo camera, wrist cameras, 4-mic array, 2× 10 W speakers |\n| Battery / runtime | 2× 18 Ah (36 Ah total) · **~4 h** runtime · 2 h charge (10–90%) · direct charge, battery swap, optional auto-recharge dock |\n| Compute (choose one) | NVIDIA Orin NX 8 GB · AGX Orin 64 GB · Thor T5000 |\n| Connectivity | Wi-Fi 2.4 / 5 GHz, Bluetooth, optional 5G · debug ports HDMI, USB 2.0, RJ45 |\n| Safety | External e-stop connector; wireless e-stop optional |\n| Development | **ROS 2 and Python SDK**; optional gamepad control |\n| Operating environment | 0–45 °C, non-condensing |\n\n- **Variants:** only \"Helios II – 5\" (5 kg arms) is specified. Compute option and most sensors are order-time choices — check what the on-site unit actually has.\n- **Typical applications:** parts handling, assembly and inspection in factories; commercial service and education / research are named targets.\n- **Hackathon notes:** indoor flat floors only (wheeled, 160 kg) — no stairs, snow or slopes. Strong fit for clinic / hotel corridor scenarios if a unit is on site.\n- Official page: [Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n\n### Human.X\n\n![Human.X](assets/rokae-human-x.jpg)\n\nFull-size bipedal humanoid with 7-DoF arms and five-finger hands. Marketing describes a VLA-based \"intelligent core\"; no developer documentation, SDK or availability statement found — treat as a showcase 🧪.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Height | **1.7 m** |\n| Weight | about **75 kg** |\n| DoF | Arm 7, leg 6, waist 2, neck 2 (per limb) |\n| Hand | 6 DoF, 12 joints, 540 g · grip force 15 N (thumb) / 10 N (other fingers), 0.5 N resolution |\n| Payload per arm | **3 kg** (excluding end effector) |\n| Joints | Fully force-controlled, dual-encoder; knee peak torque ≥320 Nm; torque / velocity / position hybrid control |\n| Walking | ≥0.5 m/s · stairs ≥15° · outdoor cement roads and standard building stairs |\n| Battery, compute, sensors | not public |\n\n- Unlikely to be available for hacking; if it is on site, expect vendor-operated demos.\n- Official page: [Humanoid Robot Human.X](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/597/Humanoid-Robot-Human.X.html)\n\n### CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)\n\n![CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)](assets/rokae-cmr.jpg)\n\nFactory mobile manipulators: a laser-SLAM AMR chassis carrying an SR or CR cobot for machine tending, material flow and inspection between workstations.\n\n| Model | Arm supported | Arm payload / reach | Chassis size / weight | Max speed | Travel | Positioning | Runtime / battery |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CMR-S-V4SD1 | SR, CR7 | 7 kg / 988 mm | 850 × 560 × 950 mm · **150 kg** | 1.4 m/s | Forward / backward | ±5 mm, ±0.5° | 12 h · 48 V 100 Ah |\n| CMR-S-V4SD3 | CR12 | 12 kg / 1434 mm | 1000 × 700 × 950 mm · 300 kg | 1.4 m/s | + traverse | ±5 mm, ±0.5° | 12 h · 48 V 100 Ah |\n| CMR-CR-V3SO6 / V3RO6 (omni, battery swap) | 7–20 kg cobots | 12 / 20 kg · 1413 mm | 1200 × 700 × 800 mm · 600 / 500 kg | 1.5 / 1.4 m/s | + traverse | ±10 mm, ±0.5° | 7 h · 100 Ah + 20 Ah, swap |\n| CMR-CR-V1SD5 (differential) | 7–20 kg cobots | 12 / 20 kg · 1413 mm | 1100 × 700 × 780 mm · 500 kg | 1.4 m/s | Forward / backward | ±10 mm, ±0.5° | 7 h · 48 V 60 Ah |\n| CMR-L-V6SOF | CR35 (35 / 45 kg) | 35 / 45 kg · 2246 / 1947 mm | 1600 × 1000 × 950 mm · **1500 kg** | 1.3 m/s | + traverse | ±10 mm, ±0.5° | 12 h empty / 8 h loaded · 48 V 200 Ah |\n\n- Navigation: laser SLAM with two diagonally mounted LiDARs. CMR-S passes 800 mm aisles.\n- **Hackathon notes:** too heavy to expect on site; listed for completeness. Developer interface for the chassis — not public.\n- Official page: [Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n\n### Intelligent Welding Robot series\n\n![Intelligent Welding Robot series](assets/rokae-welding.jpg)\n\nWelding package built on four existing cobots (SR3, SR5, CR7, CR12) with drag-teaching for small-batch welding in shipbuilding, steel structures and machinery. Arm specs are identical to the SR / CR tables above; controller is the MCC cabinet (IP54, 16 DI/DO). Welding power source and torch details — not checked. Low hackathon relevance.\n\n- Official page: [Intelligent Welding Robot](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/600/Intelligent-Welding-Robot.html)\n\n### NB / XB series\n\n![NB / XB series](assets/rokae-nb-xb.jpg)\n\nConventional fenced 6-axis industrial robots — ROKAE's original business. Not collaborative; low hackathon relevance.\n\n| Series | Payload | Reach |\n|---|---|---|\n| NB4 | 4 kg | 475–580 mm |\n| XB7 | 7 kg | 707–906 mm |\n| NB12 | 10–16 kg | 953–1602 mm |\n| NB12h (hollow wrist) | 10–12 kg | 1473–1648 mm |\n| NB25 | 12–35 kg | 1617–2110 mm |\n| NB25h, NB80, NB220 | up to **220 kg** across the range | up to **3201 mm** across the range (per-series values not checked) |\n\n- Applications: grinding, deburring, gluing, machine tending, assembly, palletizing, welding, photovoltaics.\n- Official page: [Industrial robots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/183/Industrial-Robots.html)\n\n### xCore / iNex\n\nNo picture saved (software; the docs portal offers only a logo).\n\n| Item | Status | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| **xCore** control system | ✅ | ROKAE's own controller software running every arm; user manual on the docs portal and in each product's download list (updated May 2026) |\n| **xCore SDK** | ✅ | C++, Python and C# libraries; versions 0.7.x, 0.7.x-AR (AR arms) and 0.8.x listed on the docs portal |\n| **ROS 2 package** | ✅ | Official xMate ROS 2 manual and package on the docs portal; supported distros not confirmed |\n| **Plugin development** | ✅ | Documented way to extend the controller / pendant |\n| **RokaeStudio / HMI software** | ✅ | Offline programming and pendant software (\"Software HMI\" download, MyROKAE login needed) |\n| **iNex** embodied-AI control system | 🧪 | Named in an Oct 2026 press release; no public technical detail or download |\n\n- Details and quick-start: [Developer guide](ROKAE-Developer-Guide.md) · [docs hub](https://docs.rokae.com/en/)\n\n## Accessories and ecosystem\n\n| Item | Status | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Controllers: MSC (SR), MCC (CR <35 kg, ZR), MCCM (CR ≥35 kg) | ✅ | Specs in the SR / CR sections above |\n| Compact multi-joint controller for AR arms / humanoids (drives 7, 14 or 20+ joints) | ✅ | Per profile research on the docs portal; dimensions 213 × 145.5 × 45 mm ⚠️ unverified here |\n| IO expansion: digital IO, analog IO, incremental-encoder modules | ✅ | Optional on all cobot controllers |\n| Teach pendant with drag teaching and graphical programming | ✅ | Standard cobot workflow |\n| Five-finger hand (6 DoF, 12 joints, 540 g) | 🧪 | Specified only as part of Human.X; not listed as a separate product |\n| Grippers, vacuum tools, dexterous hands for Helios | ✅ optional | Third-party brands not named — bring or ask |\n| Helios auto-recharge dock, wireless e-stop, gamepad, 5G module, extra cameras, mic array | ✅ optional | Order-time options |\n| Cameras / vision for cobots | not public | Official cases use vision, but no ROKAE-branded camera is listed |\n| Application packages: palletizing, grinding, welding | ✅ | Listed under Applications on the website |\n| Pretrained models / datasets | not public | None downloadable |\n\n## Legacy and discontinued\n\n- **xMate ER / ER Pro (7-DoF)** — no longer on the English product pages; status ⚠️ unverified (see above). Still referenced in Japanese healthcare case studies.\n- Earlier cobot generations with the controller built into the base (pre-SR / pre-CR xMate models) — implied by the SR page text; model names not confirmed.\n\n## Image credits\n\nAll images © ROKAE, downloaded from the official website on 2026-11-14 and converted to JPEG.\n\n| File | Shows | Source URL |\n|---|---|---|\n| assets/rokae-xmate-sr.jpg | xMate SR series header image | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20261111/b21f485ef496fb8e9ae7ff1d71641a37.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-xmate-cr.jpg | xMate CR series banner | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20240129/c5cae36ae4f5beaef1bdb39a766939a7.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-xmate-zr.jpg | xMate ZR series banner | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20261029/ac3247c2ddd368ac3bcce4084eec34b3.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-ar.jpg | AR humanoid force-controlled arm header image | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20261110/1f3c633ff451f6fe94afc8afd4c7bdd7.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-hsa.jpg | HSA joint modules header image | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20260107/22043ea92487d19ca823a3c2fe62123b.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-helios.jpg | Helios wheeled dual-arm robot header image | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20260918/14005af60c70ff620c9840a78b81af4b.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-human-x.jpg | Human.X humanoid header image | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20251114/c8dc81cd7a77852d4b3c7d7e0bb368e4.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-cmr.jpg | CMR mobile manipulator header image | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20261111/154f6d3fb8d9420a5a3614d14bb82fd3.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-welding.jpg | Intelligent Welding Robot header image | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20251114/de735fada1f3e6b53a978b4e16c06114.jpg |\n| assets/rokae-nb-xb.jpg | Industrial robots (NB / XB) category header | https://www.rokae.com/public/uploads/20230206/8470eecb025b19bfb55cea15e9b8904e.jpg |\n\nNot saved: `rokae-xmate-er-pro` (no current official product page) and `rokae-xcore` (software, no product image).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — xMate ZR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/626/xMate-ZR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Integrated Force-Controlled Joint HSA](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/598/Integrated-Force-Controlled-Joint-HSA.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Robot Human.X](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/597/Humanoid-Robot-Human.X.html)\n- [ROKAE — Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Intelligent Welding Robot](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/600/Intelligent-Welding-Robot.html)\n- [ROKAE — Industrial Robots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/183/Industrial-Robots.html)\n- [ROKAE — Collaborative Robots overview](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/182/Collaborative-Robots.html)\n- [ROKAE documentation hub](https://docs.rokae.com/en/)\n- [ROKAE Japan — teleoperated ultrasound robot case (ER7 Pro)](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1189/%E9%81%A0%E9%9A%94%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C%E5%9E%8B%E8%B6%85%E9%9F%B3%E6%B3%A2%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%83%BB%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html)\n- [ROKAE Robotics profile (this repo) — deployment, status and SDK facts carried over](ROKAE.md)\n","ROKAE-Roadmap-and-Strategy":"---\ntype: roadmap\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, roadmap]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics — Roadmap and Strategy\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](ROKAE.md) · [Product portfolio](ROKAE-Products.md) · [Developer guide](ROKAE-Developer-Guide.md)\n\n> Research note: based on ROKAE's own news pages, its Oct 2026 press release, the IPO sponsor-side announcement and the official docs / GitHub. Open web search was not available for this pass, so trade-press coverage, pre-IPO funding rounds and European channel details are thin and marked where unconfirmed.\n\n**In one line:** a force-control specialist that went public in July 2026 and is repositioning from \"cobot maker\" to **supplier of force-controlled arms, joints and controllers for other companies' humanoids**, with its own wheeled humanoid (Helios) as the reference platform.\n\n## Company trajectory\n\n| Date | Milestone |\n|---|---|\n| 2014 | Company founded (per Oct 2026 press release); founder and CEO **Tuo Hua** |\n| 2014–2024 | Builds industrial arms (NB / XB) and xMate force-controlled cobots on its own xCore controller. Pre-IPO venture rounds: details not verified in this research |\n| Aug 2024 | Python SDK v0.4.1 adds force-control interfaces (xCore 2.2.2) |\n| Dec 2024 | SDK v0.5.0 for xCore 3.0.1 |\n| Jan–Jun 2026 | SDK 0.7.0 → 0.7.2 for xCore 3.2.x; separate 0.7.x-AR docs branch for the humanoid arm; 0.8.x docs appear |\n| 9 Jul 2026 | **Hong Kong Main Board listing (3752)** — about 23M shares at HKD 38, raising about **HKD 875M** |\n| ~Jul 2026 | Human.X full-size bipedal humanoid launched 🧪 (date from a single secondary source — ⚠️ unverified) |\n| Aug 2026 | Strategic partnership with **SHANTUI** (construction machinery) on intelligent construction equipment (news listed 10 Aug 2026) |\n| 19 Aug 2026 | **World Robot Conference 2026, Beijing:** \"full-stack development platform for embodied intelligence\" — HSA joints + AR arms + Helios + **iNex** controller |\n| Sep 2026 | **10,000th AR humanoid force-controlled arm** leaves the Shandong production base |\n| 27 Oct 2026 | Press release: 10,000 arms, **80+ industrial partners**, five Helios robots running at **Schaeffler** Taicang |\n| Nov 2026 (listing dates) | Bosch China supplier innovation award; Shandong Heavy Industry Group visit; **Northeast Intelligent Manufacturing Alliance** launched in Shenyang with three integrators |\n\nDates on ROKAE's English news list are posting dates and can trail the event by weeks.\n\n## Current focus projects\n\n| Focus | What they are doing now | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Humanoid arms as a component business | AR 7-DoF arms sold to other robot makers; counted separately from cobots | 10,000 units, 80+ partners (Oct 2026) |\n| Reference humanoid in real factories | Helios wheeled dual-arm robots doing parts handling, assembly and inspection at Schaeffler's Taicang lighthouse factory | Five units deployed |\n| Unified whole-body control | **iNex** 🧪: one real-time controller for arms, waist, head and chassis; whole-body control, impedance / admittance / hybrid force-position; hooks for teleoperation devices and model training | WRC 2026 news; no public API |\n| Learned manipulation skills | Dual-arm AR5 USB insertion with imitation learning + human-in-the-loop RL, reported 95–99 % success | WRC 2026 demo; model not released |\n| Teleoperation data collection | Demonstrated with third-party gear: IO-AI TeleXperience, Wuji Hand, motion capture, exoskeleton gloves | WRC 2026 demo |\n| Welding | AI-assisted cobot welding packages, shown at several 2026 Chinese trade events | Aug and Nov 2026 news |\n| Channel building in China | \"ROKAE+\" open ecosystem; regional integrator alliances (laser, grinding / polishing, general automation) | Nov 2026 alliance news |\n| Wellness / therapy applications | Therapeutic massage robot with partner EaseFuture (acupoint detection in about 3 s) shown at WRC 2026 | WRC 2026 news |\n\n## Announced and upcoming products\n\n| Item | Status | Date | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| iNex embodied-AI / whole-body control platform | 🧪 | Shown Aug 2026 | Called an \"open motion-control platform\"; no public docs, SDK or pricing |\n| Human.X bipedal humanoid | 🧪 | Mid-2026 | Product page exists; availability and price not public |\n| Full-stack embodied development platform (HSA + AR + Helios + iNex) | 🧪 | Aug 2026 | Positioned as ready-to-use for partners; commercial terms not public |\n| Larger AR arm (up to 20 kg payload) | 🧪 ⚠️ unverified | Aug 2026 | WRC news quotes an AR payload range of 3–20 kg; the product page lists AR3 / AR5 / AR10 |\n| Helios with \"43 DoF\" configuration | 🧪 ⚠️ unverified | Aug 2026 | News says 43 DoF; product spec table says 23 (likely with vs without hands) |\n| SDK 0.8.x | ✅ docs published | 2026 | Changes versus 0.7.x not checked |\n| VLA / foundation model from ROKAE | 🔭 | — | Mentioned in Human.X marketing only; nothing downloadable |\n| Dated product roadmap beyond 2026 | not public | — | — |\n\n## Strategic direction and markets\n\n- **Positioning:** \"Robotics + AI\" across three lines — industrial robots, flexible collaborative robots, embodied intelligent robots — and across three layers: core components (HSA joints), robot bodies (arms, Helios, Human.X), control systems (xCore, iNex). The stated aim is that partners build on ROKAE's platform instead of redeveloping force control.\n- **Differentiator:** joint-torque-sensor force control developed in-house for over a decade; CEO messaging is that force control \"has no shortcuts\".\n- **Market position (CIC data cited at IPO):** top six among cobot providers in China; top five in China's embodied robotic-arm market.\n- **Customers:** **1,000+ enterprises in 40+ countries and regions**. Named industrial references in 2026: Schaeffler, Bosch (supplier award), SHANTUI, Shandong Heavy Industry Group (talks).\n- **Funding:** IPO proceeds of about HKD 875M (Jul 2026). Use-of-proceeds breakdown not confirmed here. Earlier private rounds — not verified. A net loss was guided for 2026 (see profile).\n- **Go-to-market:** integrator and distributor led. In China, regional alliances (\"headquarters empowerment + platform distribution + specialised integration\").\n- **Europe / Switzerland:** sold through distributors (for example a Danish reseller, see profile). No Swiss entity, Swiss deployment or European R&D site found — **not public**. Participation in a Swiss hackathon is itself a signal of interest in European developers and research labs (inference).\n- **Japan:** localised website with healthcare case studies (moxibustion, tele-ultrasound).\n- **Risks to weigh as a builder:** public developer material covers arms only; Helios, Human.X and iNex have no open documentation; small open-source community; English docs are partly machine-translated.\n\n## Research and open-source activity\n\n| Area | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Open-source code | Official GitHub account `RokaeRobot` (8 public repos): xCoreSDK C++ / Python / C# / Android, `rokae_ros2`, two xCore plugin kits. Apache-2.0. Active in 2026 (plugin repos updated Oct–Nov 2026, SDKs Jul 2026) |\n| Community size | Small — xCoreSDK-CPP about 46 stars, rokae_ros2 about 12 |\n| Simulation assets | URDF / xacro + Gazebo + MoveIt 2 configs in `rokae_ros2`; no official MuJoCo or Isaac assets found |\n| Models and datasets | None released |\n| Publications | Not checked in this pass. xMate 7-DoF arms are used in university force-control research — ⚠️ unverified, no specific papers confirmed |\n| Embodied-AI research shown publicly | Imitation learning + human-in-the-loop RL for precision assembly; teleoperation data capture with partners (WRC 2026) |\n\n## What would impress this company at the hackathon\n\n**Inference, not a statement from ROKAE.** It follows from what they promote: force control, arms as a platform for others, real industrial and wellness use cases.\n\n| Track | Build that plays to their strategy | Why it would land |\n|---|---|---|\n| T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation | Compliant physiotherapy or thermal-spa assistant: guided limb mobilisation or massage stroke with adjustable Cartesian stiffness, force limits and logged force / range-of-motion data for the therapist | Mirrors their massage and therapy demos; shows force control doing something position control cannot; fits Leukerbad's rehab clinics |\n| T04 | Teleoperated or demonstration-taught care task (drag-teach once, replay with force adaptation), ideally with a dexterous hand from another vendor on site | Matches their teleoperation + data-collection narrative and the Wuji Hand pairing they already showed |\n| T01 Autonomous Urban Services | ROKAE arm mounted on another vendor's mobile base or quadruped for a contact task: pressing lift buttons, opening doors, handing over parcels with force-sensed release | Demonstrates the \"arm as a component for other robots\" business they are pushing |\n| T02 Resilient & Safe City | Remote contact inspection or valve / switch operation with haptic-style force feedback from joint torque sensing | Uses torque sensing as a sensor, not just for safety |\n| T03 Sustainable City | Force-guided disassembly or sorting (unscrewing, connector removal, pressing recyclables apart) learned from a few kinesthetic demos | Close to their USB-insertion / precision-assembly showcase, with a sustainability outcome |\n\nCross-cutting points likely to score:\n\n- **Use the force data visibly** — plots of contact force, stiffness changes, collision reactions.\n- **Stay on the public stack** (xCore SDK, `rokae_ros2`) and contribute something reusable: a LeRobot wrapper, an MJCF / USD model, a ROS 2 force-control node. Their open-source footprint is small, so a clean contribution stands out.\n- **Show a path to a real customer** (clinic, hotel, municipal service) rather than a lab trick — their messaging is about deployment at scale.\n- **Safety discipline** around people: low stiffness, speed limits, e-stop, dummy testing first.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — ROKAE delivers its 10,000th humanoid force-controlled arm, 27 Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n- [Deloitte China — listing of Rokae Robotics on SEHK, Jul 2026](https://www.deloitte.com/cn/en/about/press-room/deloitte-china-assists-listing-of-rokae-robotics-on-sehk.html)\n- [ROKAE news index](https://www.rokae.com/en/news/)\n- [ROKAE news — WRC 2026 full-stack development platform for embodied intelligence](https://www.rokae.com/en/news/show/2604/WRC-2026:-ROKAE-Unveils-a-Ready-to-Use-Full-Stack-Development-Platform-for-Embodied-Intelligence,-Advancing-Physical-AI-Infrastructure-Development.html)\n- [ROKAE news — Northeast Intelligent Manufacturing Alliance](https://www.rokae.com/en/news/show/2608/ROKAE-Robotics-Officially-Launches-Northeast-Intelligent-Manufacturing-Alliance-Under-Its-New-Ecosystem-to-Empower-Industrial-Collaboration.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE SDK introduction](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/SDK_Intro)\n- [ROKAE C++ SDK introduction (version history)](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/cpp/cpp_Intro)\n- [ROKAE Python SDK introduction (version history)](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/python/py_Intro)\n- [Rokae xMate ROS 2 user manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2/rokae%20ros2%E4%BD%BF%E7%94%A8%E6%89%8B%E5%86%8C)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot account](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [ROKAE Robotics profile (this repo) — IPO, customers, Europe distributor and Human.X sources](ROKAE.md)\n","ROKAE":"---\ntype: company\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nhq: \"Beijing, China (listed entity registered in Shandong Province)\"\nfounded: 2014\nwebsite: https://www.rokae.com/en/\ncategories: [arm, mobile-manipulator, humanoid, components, software]\non_site_guess: \"xMate force-controlled cobot arms (SR / CR class) with xCore SDK; possibly an AR 7-DoF humanoid arm or a Helios wheeled dual-arm robot\"\nfit_t01: 3\nfit_t02: 2\nfit_t03: 3\nfit_t04: 4\nidentity_confidence: high\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, company]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics\n\n> **One-liner:** Chinese robot maker (HKEX: 3752) selling industrial arms, force-controlled \"flexible\" collaborative arms and, since 2025–26, embodied-AI hardware: 7-DoF humanoid arms, force-controlled joints, a wheeled dual-arm robot and a bipedal humanoid.\n> **Why it matters at Leukerbad:** ROKAE's core skill is joint-torque force control — the capability you need for anything that touches a person or an unknown surface (massage, physiotherapy, guided ultrasound, compliant handling). It is the most natural arm vendor for a contact-rich T04 build, and a solid manipulation component for T01/T03.\n\n## Snapshot\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| HQ | Beijing, China (press datelines, \"ROKAE (Beijing) Robotics Co., LTD\" on the website). Listed entity: **Rokae (Shandong) Robotics Group Inc.**; manufacturing base in Shandong Province |\n| Founded | **2014** per the company's Oct 2026 press release (2015 appears in some older third-party profiles) |\n| Founders / CEO | **Tuo Hua**, Founder and CEO |\n| Size and funding | Listed on the Hong Kong Main Board on **9 Jul 2026** (code **3752**), raising about **HKD 875M** at HKD 38/share. **1,000+ enterprise customers**, **40+ countries/regions**. Headcount: not public in the sources used. Company guided to a net loss for 2026 (investor commentary) |\n| Core business | Industrial robots (NB / XB), flexible collaborative robots (xMate CR / SR / ZR), embodied intelligent robots (AR arms, HSA joints, Helios, Human.X, CMR mobile manipulators, welding robots) |\n| Website | https://www.rokae.com/en/ |\n| Docs / developer portal | https://docs.rokae.com/en/ (xCore manual, SDK, ROS 2 package manual, plugin development, downloads) |\n| GitHub | No official org confirmed in this research. SDK and ROS 2 packages are distributed via the docs portal downloads. ⚠️ unverified: a `RokaeRobot` GitHub account hosting xCoreSDK repos |\n\n## Portfolio at a glance\n\n| Product | Type | Status | Key specs | Price | Img key |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| xMate SR series (SR3, SR5) | Compact 6-axis force-controlled cobot | ✅ | **3 / 5 kg** payload · **705 / 919 mm** reach · ~13.8 / 16.5 kg · ±0.03 mm · 48 VDC · IP54 · Cartesian stiffness adjustable 0–3000 N/m · 1.2 kg MSC controller | not public | rokae-xmate-sr |\n| xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35) | 6-axis (some 5-axis) cobot | ✅ | **5–45 kg** payload · **988–2246 mm** reach · joint torque sensing for collision detection · IP67 (site-wide claim) · cabinet-less option | not public | rokae-xmate-cr |\n| xMate ZR series (ZR6) | 6-axis cobot | ✅ | **6 kg** · **980 mm** · other specs not checked | not public | rokae-xmate-zr |\n| xMate ER Pro (ER3 Pro / ER7 Pro) | 7-DoF cobot with joint torque sensors | ⚠️ unverified status (legacy line; not on the current English cobot page, still in Japanese case studies) | ER7 Pro: **7 kg** · **850 mm** · **7 DoF** | not public | rokae-xmate-er-pro |\n| AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10) | 7-DoF humanoid force-controlled arm | ✅ (10,000th unit delivered, Sep–Oct 2026) | **3 / 5 / 10 kg** payload · **650 / 683 / 815 mm** reach · **8 / 10 / 19.5 kg** arm weight · joint torque sensors standard · force resolution ≤0.05 N · EtherCAT 1 ms cycle · 48 VDC · IP54 | not public | rokae-ar |\n| HSA series (HSA-11 … HSA-32) | Integrated force-controlled joint module | ✅ | Peak torque **11–459 Nm** · weight **0.45–6.0 kg** · torque sensor resolution ≤0.05 Nm · 1 kHz force loop · EtherCAT · 48 V | not public | rokae-hsa |\n| Helios (Helios II) | Wheeled dual-arm humanoid | ✅ (deployed at Schaeffler) | **23 DoF** in spec table (intro text says 42) · **5 kg per arm** · omni chassis **1.5 m/s** · height 1493–2000 mm · **160 kg** · ~4 h runtime · 2× 3D LiDAR + head stereo camera · Orin NX / AGX Orin / Thor options · ROS 2 + Python SDK | not public | rokae-helios |\n| Human.X | Full-size bipedal humanoid | 🧪 (launched mid-2026; availability not stated) | **1.7 m** · ~**75 kg** · 7-DoF arms, **3 kg per arm** · walking ≥0.5 m/s · 6-DoF hands (12 joints, 540 g) | not public | rokae-human-x |\n| CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR) | Mobile manipulator (AMR + cobot) | ✅ | Arm payload **7–45 kg** by model · max **1.3–1.5 m/s** · laser SLAM, 2 LiDARs · docking ±5 to ±10 mm · 7–12 h operation · chassis 150–1500 kg | not public | rokae-cmr |\n| Intelligent Welding Robot series | Cobot welding package | ✅ | Specs not checked | not public | rokae-welding |\n| NB / XB series (NB4, XB7, NB12, NB12h, NB25, NB25h, NB80, NB220) | 6-axis industrial robots | ✅ | **4–220 kg** payload · **475–3201 mm** reach | not public | rokae-nb-xb |\n| xCore / iNex | Control system (xCore) and embodied-AI control system (iNex) | ✅ xCore · 🧪 iNex (named in press release, no public technical detail found) | xCore manual V3.2; compact MAC controller (213 × 145.5 × 45 mm) drives 7, 14 or 20+ joints | bundled | rokae-xcore |\n\n## What they will most likely bring to the hackathon\n\n**Best guess, not confirmed — check the vendor table on site.**\n\n- **Most likely: xMate SR3 / SR5 or a small CR (CR7 / CR12).** These are the 48 V, light, cabinet-light cobots ROKAE shows at trade fairs and uses for commercial demos (massage, coffee, picking). They ship by air easily and work with the public SDK.\n- **Plausible: AR-series 7-DoF arm(s), single or dual.** This is ROKAE's 2026 headline product (10,000 delivered) and it has its own SDK branch (0.7.x-AR). A dual-AR bench rig would fit an \"embodied AI\" hackathon.\n- **Possible but heavier logistics: Helios wheeled dual-arm robot** (160 kg). If one is on site it is the only ROKAE platform that covers mobility plus manipulation out of the box.\n- **Unlikely:** Human.X biped (new, availability unclear), NB/XB industrial arms, large CMR units (500–1500 kg chassis).\n- **Bring-your-own parts to plan for:** gripper or tool, camera, and a Linux PC for the SDK — none of these are confirmed as supplied.\n\n## Developer platform (summary)\n\n- **SDK (\"xCore SDK\"):** documented for **C++, Python and C#** on docs.rokae.com; versions listed are **0.7.x, 0.7.x-AR (humanoid arm) and 0.8.x**. Includes quick start, error codes, FAQ and a table mapping SDK calls to pendant (HMI) functions.\n- **Real-time mode:** the C++ docs contain real-time-mode troubleshooting, i.e. a low-level streaming interface exists for C++. Exact rate and available modes (joint position / Cartesian impedance / torque) were not confirmed here — ⚠️ unverified: 1 kHz real-time control with impedance and direct torque modes. Hardware side: AR joints run a 1 ms EtherCAT cycle and HSA joints quote a 1 kHz force loop.\n- **High-level control:** non-real-time motion commands via SDK; pendant with drag teaching and graphical flow programming; **RokaeStudio** offline programming.\n- **ROS 2:** an official \"Rokae xMate ROS 2 User Manual\" and package exist on the docs portal. Supported distros, MoveIt and ros2_control details not confirmed. Helios lists **ROS 2 + Python SDK** for secondary development.\n- **Plugins:** a plugin-development section exists for extending the controller/HMI.\n- **Simulation assets:** URDFs are expected with the ROS 2 package; official MuJoCo / Isaac assets — not public / not found.\n- **Teleoperation / data collection:** a remote-ultrasound case uses ER7 Pro under teleoperation; Helios lists only gamepad control. No public data-collection toolkit or dataset found.\n- **Pretrained models:** none public. Human.X marketing mentions a VLA \"intelligent core\" and the press release names the iNex embodied-AI control system, without downloadable models. Plan to bring your own policy (for example LeRobot / OpenVLA-style) or pair with another vendor's VLA.\n- **On-board compute:** cobots use the xCore controller (no GPU) — add an external PC. Helios offers Jetson Orin NX 8 GB, AGX Orin 64 GB or Thor T5000.\n- **Links:** [docs hub](https://docs.rokae.com/en/) · [SDK docs](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK) · [ROS 2 manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2) · [download center](https://www.rokae.com/en/service/190/Download-Center.html). Some spec sheets require a MyROKAE login.\n\n## Current projects, deployments and customers\n\n- **Oct 2026** — 10,000th AR humanoid force-controlled arm delivered; **80+ industrial partners** have integrated the arms; **1,000+ enterprise customers** overall ([TechNode Global / PR Newswire](https://technode.global/?p=996114)).\n- **Oct 2026** — **Schaeffler** Taicang lighthouse factory runs **five Helios** wheeled humanoids with AR arms for parts handling, assembly and inspection on a humanoid-component line ([same release](https://technode.global/?p=996114)).\n- **Jul 2026** — Hong Kong IPO; prospectus cites customers in **40+ countries and regions**, top-six position among Chinese cobot providers and top-five in embodied robotic arms (CIC data quoted by [Deloitte China](https://www.deloitte.com/cn/en/about/press-room/deloitte-china-assists-listing-of-rokae-robotics-on-sehk.html)).\n- **Q1 2026** — 5,100+ robots shipped in the quarter and confirmed orders of RMB 577.3M at 31 Mar 2026, per secondary IPO analysis (⚠️ unverified against the prospectus).\n- **Healthcare / wellness (undated case studies on rokae.com):** AI massage robot on SR arms; [moxibustion therapy robot](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1196/%E3%81%8A%E7%81%B8%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html) (xMate + vision); [teleoperated ultrasound diagnosis](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1189/%E9%81%A0%E9%9A%94%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C%E5%9E%8B%E8%B6%85%E9%9F%B3%E6%B3%A2%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%83%BB%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html) with xMate ER7 Pro. These are integrator applications, not certified ROKAE medical devices.\n- **Commercial services (SR product page):** robotic coffee and ice cream, fruit picking, chemical catalyst preparation.\n- **Europe:** sold through distributors (for example ZTEC in Denmark lists xMate cobots). No Swiss deployment or subsidiary found — not public.\n\n## Roadmap and future direction\n\n- **Jul 2026** — IPO proceeds (~HKD 875M) raised; detailed use of proceeds not confirmed in the sources used.\n- **Mid-2026** — \"Embodied Intelligent Robot\" line established as a third business pillar: AR arms, HSA joints, Helios, Human.X, CMR, welding. Human.X bipedal humanoid reported launched around **Jul 2026** (single secondary source plus official product page) 🧪.\n- **Oct–Nov 2026** — Messaging centres on being a force-control component and arm supplier to other humanoid makers (10,000 arms, 80+ partners) rather than only a full-robot brand.\n- **Nov 2026** — Company news mentions a \"Northeast Intelligent Manufacturing Alliance\" launch (headline only; details not checked).\n- **iNex embodied-AI control system** 🧪 and VLA-based \"intelligent core\" 🔭 — named, no public developer access found.\n- Profitability: net loss expected for 2026 per investor commentary — treat long-term support promises accordingly.\n\n## Challenge fit\n\n| Track | Fit 0–5 | Why (one line) | Best products | Go deeper |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T01 Autonomous Urban Services | 3 | Good manipulation half of a service robot (kiosk, handover, maintenance tasks); mobility only if Helios or CMR is on site, otherwise pair with another vendor's base | xMate SR / CR, Helios, CMR-S | [T01 brief](T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/ROKAE-T01-1-Brief.md) |\n| T02 Resilient & Safe City | 2 | Arms are indoor, fixed-base tools; useful for remote manipulation or inspection contact tasks, not for terrain or hazard response | xMate CR (IP67 claim), AR arm on another vendor's mobile base | [T02 brief](T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/ROKAE-T02-1-Brief.md) |\n| T03 Sustainable City | 3 | Standard cobot path to waste sorting, disassembly, picking or lab automation with a camera; nothing sustainability-specific from ROKAE | xMate SR5 / CR7 / CR12, CMR | [T03 brief](T03-Sustainable-City/ROKAE-T03-1-Brief.md) |\n| T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation | 4 | Force control with adjustable stiffness and existing massage, moxibustion and tele-ultrasound applications give a clear 48-hour path to a safe contact demo | xMate SR3 / SR5, AR5, ER7 Pro (if available) | [T04 brief](T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/ROKAE-T04-1-Brief.md) |\n\n## Related docs\n- [Product portfolio](ROKAE-Products.md) · [Developer guide](ROKAE-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](ROKAE-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n- [Start here — all companies and tracks](../../00-START-HERE.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE official site (EN) — lineup, navigation, news](https://www.rokae.com/en/)\n- [ROKAE — Collaborative Robots overview](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/182/Collaborative-Robots.html)\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Robot Human.X](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/597/Humanoid-Robot-Human.X.html)\n- [ROKAE — Integrated Force-Controlled Joint HSA](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/598/Integrated-Force-Controlled-Joint-HSA.html)\n- [ROKAE — Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n- [ROKAE documentation hub](https://docs.rokae.com/en/)\n- [ROKAE SDK documentation](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [ROKAE ROS 2 package manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — ROKAE delivers its 10,000th humanoid force-controlled arm, 27 Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n- [Deloitte China — listing of Rokae Robotics on SEHK, Jul 2026](https://www.deloitte.com/cn/en/about/press-room/deloitte-china-assists-listing-of-rokae-robotics-on-sehk.html)\n- [Origin of Bots — ROKAE launches Human.X](https://www.originofbots.com/news/rokae-launches-humanx-bipedal-humanoid-robot-for-industrial-applications)\n- [Minichart — Rokae IPO analysis (orders, Q1 2026 shipments)](https://www.minichart.com.sg/2026/07/05/rokae-shandong-robotics-group-inc-ipo-analysis-financial-outlook-broker-coverage-and-listing-prospects/)\n- [iTiger / Deep News — key investor concerns for Rokae IPO](https://www.itiger.com/news/1197773788)\n- [ROKAE Japan — moxibustion therapy robot case](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1196/%E3%81%8A%E7%81%B8%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html)\n- [ROKAE Japan — teleoperated ultrasound robot case (ER7 Pro)](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1189/%E9%81%A0%E9%9A%94%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C%E5%9E%8B%E8%B6%85%E9%9F%B3%E6%B3%A2%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%83%BB%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html)\n- [ZTEC (Denmark) — ROKAE cobot reseller listing](https://www.ztec.dk/en/cobots)\n","ROKAE-T01-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T01\nchallenge_title: \"Autonomous Urban Services\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, brief]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T01 Autonomous Urban Services — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — Strong force-controlled *manipulation* half of a service robot with an open SDK; the \"moves through the city\" half must come from Helios / CMR (if on site) or another vendor's base.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **xMate SR3 / SR5** (48 VDC, 13.8 / 16.5 kg, 1.2 kg controller, IP54) or **CR7** (IP67) as a fixed service station; **Helios** if one is on site (⚠️ unverified what ROKAE brings) |\n| Strongest proof point | SR cobots in **unmanned catering, robotic coffee and ice-cream** kiosks (official case studies, undated); **5 Helios** at Schaeffler Taicang (Oct 2026) — factory, not city |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Unattended service kiosk / hand-off dock: camera + SR arm picks an ordered item and hands it to a guest or loads it into another vendor's delivery robot, using Cartesian impedance for safe contact |\n| Biggest risk | No mobility unless Helios / CMR is present; gripper, camera and PC are not confirmed as supplied; CMR / Helios developer interfaces are thinly documented |\n| Pairs well with | Unitree / AGIBOT / Genisom quadruped (delivery leg) · D-Robotics RDK (edge vision) · WUJI hand or Flexiv Grav gripper (end effector) · SKYLANDX (site map) |\n\n## Why\n- **Open, real developer stack:** Apache-2.0 `xCoreSDK` (C++ / Python / C#) and `rokae_ros2` (ROS 2 Humble, MoveIt 2 configs, URDFs, Gazebo) on GitHub — rare among arm vendors here.\n- **1 kHz real-time C++ control** with joint / Cartesian impedance and direct torque on cobots — enables contact tasks (button pressing, wiping, door handles, handover).\n- **Proven public-facing service use** (coffee, ice cream, unmanned catering), i.e. arms already run unattended near customers.\n- **Light, 48 VDC SR arms** can ride on a third-party mobile base — with integration work.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Outdoor street navigation, snow, stairs or steep terrain — ROKAE has no outdoor mobile platform.\n- Teams wanting an out-of-the-box delivery or cleaning robot, or a ready VLA model (none public).\n- Pure navigation / fleet projects with no manipulation step.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html) · [xMate CR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html) · [Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — Commercial Services cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/208/Commercial-Services.html)\n- [GitHub — xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP) · [rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — Schaeffler Helios deployment, Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T01-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/T01-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","ROKAE-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, products]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T01 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T01-3-Playbook.md)\n\n> **Read this first:** what ROKAE actually has on site is **⚠️ unverified**. The ranking below assumes the likely case (a small xMate cobot) and treats Helios / CMR as a bonus. Confirm at the vendor table before committing.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)\n\n![xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)](../assets/rokae-xmate-sr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the service-kiosk and hand-off arm. Light enough to mount on a counter, a cart or (with work) another vendor's mobile base; this is the arm ROKAE itself uses in coffee, ice-cream and unmanned-catering cases.\n\n| Spec | SR3 | SR5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** |\n| Reach | **705 mm** | **919 mm** |\n| Arm weight | ~13.8 kg | ~16.5 kg |\n| Max TCP speed | ≤1.5 m/s | ≤2.0 m/s |\n| Repeatability | ±0.03 mm | ±0.03 mm |\n| Protection | IP54 (arm) · IP20 (controller) | same |\n| Power | **48 VDC** | **48 VDC** |\n| Operating temperature | 0–50 °C | 0–50 °C |\n| Cartesian stiffness (adjustable) | 0–3000 N/m | 0–3000 N/m |\n| Force-control accuracy (relative) | 0.5 N / 0.1 Nm | 0.1 N / 0.02 Nm |\n| Controller (MSC) | 203 × 175 × 42 mm, ~1.2 kg, 5 DI / 5 DO, EtherCAT, 3× GbE, RS485 | same |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ✅ shipping · price not public · on-site availability ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Developer control:** xCore SDK (C++ / Python / C#); non-real-time moves, I/O, drag teaching and path replay, collision detection settings; **real-time C++ up to 1 kHz** with joint position, Cartesian position, joint impedance, Cartesian impedance and direct torque. ROS 2 Humble + MoveIt 2 config packages exist for SR3 / SR4 / SR5.\n\n### xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)\n\n![xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)](../assets/rokae-xmate-cr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the sturdier, wash-down-capable station arm — relevant where the task is wet or dirty (spa surroundings, cleaning, waste handling) or needs more reach than an SR.\n\n| Spec | CR7 | CR12 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **7 kg** | **12 kg** |\n| Reach | **988 mm** | **1434 mm** |\n| Arm weight | ~25 kg | ~41 kg |\n| Protection (arm) | **IP67** | **IP67** |\n| Max tool speed | ≤3.2 m/s | ≤3.0 m/s |\n| Cartesian stiffness (adjustable) | 0–6000 N/m | 0–18000 N/m |\n| Force-control accuracy (relative) | 0.5 N / 0.1 Nm | 0.5 N / 0.1 Nm |\n| Controller (MCC) | IP54, ~16.6 kg, single-phase 90–264 V AC | same |\n\n- **Note:** CR5 is IP54, not IP67. The 16.6 kg AC-powered cabinet makes a CR harder to put on a small third-party mobile base than an SR.\n- **Status / price / availability:** ✅ shipping · price not public · on-site availability ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Developer control:** same xCore SDK and real-time modes as SR; MoveIt 2 configs for CR7 / CR12 / CR18 / CR20 / CR35.\n\n### Helios (Helios II)\n\n![Helios (Helios II)](../assets/rokae-helios.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the only ROKAE platform that both drives and manipulates — an indoor concierge / fetch-and-deliver / facility-upkeep robot. Only relevant if one is physically on site.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Arms | 2 × 7-DoF, **5 kg per arm** |\n| DoF (spec table) | 23 (2 neck, 7 + 7 arms, 2 waist, 2 legs, 3 chassis); intro text says 42 — inconsistent on the official page |\n| Chassis | Omnidirectional, 4 drive wheels, **1.5 m/s**, docking ±10 mm |\n| Size / weight | 750 × 650 × 1493–2000 mm · **160 kg** |\n| Runtime | ~**4 h** (2 × 18 Ah); charge 10–90 % in ~2 h; battery swap; optional auto-recharge dock |\n| Sensors | 2 × 3D LiDAR, head stereo camera, IMU; optional fisheye ×3, chest and wrist cameras, 4-mic array, speaker |\n| Compute (one of) | Jetson Orin NX 8 GB · AGX Orin 64 GB · Thor T5000 |\n| Environment | 0–45 °C, non-condensing; IP rating, slope and threshold-crossing ability not listed |\n| End effectors (optional) | Electric gripper, dexterous hand, vacuum gripper |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ✅ deployed in a factory (Schaeffler, Oct 2026) · price not public · on-site availability ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Developer control:** product page states **ROS 2 + Python SDK** and optional gamepad. Public API docs, navigation stack details and simulation assets for Helios — not public / not found. Helios is not among the models in the public `rokae_ros2` repo.\n\n### CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)\n\n![CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)](../assets/rokae-cmr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** AMR + cobot for indoor material runs (facility logistics, restocking, machine tending). Conceptually the closest ROKAE product to \"robots that move and deliver\", but built for factory floors and heavy.\n\n| Spec | CMR-S-V4SD1 (smallest) | Range across series |\n|---|---|---|\n| Arm payload | 7 kg (SR series, CR7, CR12 compatible) | 7–45 kg |\n| Max speed | **1.4 m/s** | 1.3–1.5 m/s |\n| Footprint | 850 × 560 × 950 mm, min. aisle 800 mm | up to 1600 × 1000 mm |\n| Chassis weight | 150 kg | 150–1500 kg |\n| Navigation | Laser SLAM, 2 LiDARs (front / rear diagonal) | same |\n| Positioning accuracy | ±5 mm, ±0.5° | ±5 to ±10 mm |\n| Runtime | 12 h (48 V, 100 Ah) | 7–12 h |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ✅ shipping · price not public · unlikely on site (weight) ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Developer control:** not public. The page lists no API, fleet interface, IP rating, slope or obstacle specs.\n\n### AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)\n\n![AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)](../assets/rokae-ar.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** 7-DoF torque-sensing arm designed to be mounted on someone else's robot body — the natural ROKAE part for a cross-vendor mobile manipulator.\n\n| Spec | AR3 | AR5 | AR10 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Payload | 3 kg | 5 kg | 10 kg |\n| Reach | 650 mm | 683 mm | 815 mm |\n| Arm weight | 8 kg | 10 kg | 19.5 kg |\n| Common | 7 DoF · joint torque sensors · force resolution ≤0.05 N · EtherCAT 1 ms · 48 VDC · IP54 | | |\n\n- **Status / price / availability:** ✅ shipping (10,000th unit delivered, Oct 2026) · price not public · on-site availability ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Developer control:** dedicated SDK branch (0.7.x-AR); MoveIt 2 configs for AR5L / AR5R in `rokae_ros2`.\n\n### xCore / iNex\n\n![xCore / iNex](../assets/rokae-xcore.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the software you actually program. xCore (controller **v3.2.1 or later** is required by the public SDK and ROS 2 package) exposes motion, force control and I/O. iNex 🧪 (embodied-AI control system) has no public developer access.\n\n**Lower priority for T01:** Human.X biped 🧪 (availability unclear), HSA joints (component-level, not a 48-hour path), NB / XB industrial arms and welding packages (fenced industrial use), xMate ZR and ER Pro (no track-specific advantage; ER Pro status ⚠️ unverified).\n\n## What ROKAE Robotics already does in this domain\n\nHonest summary: **commercial-service kiosks yes; city-scale mobile service robots no.**\n\n| When | What | Relevance to T01 | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Undated case study | xMate SR cobot in **robotic coffee** | Unattended hospitality / retail kiosk | [rokae.com](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/1089/xMate-SR-Cobot-in-Robotic-Coffee.html) |\n| Undated case study | xMate SR cobot in **robotic ice cream** | Same | [rokae.com](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/1090/xMate-SR-Cobot-in-Robotic-Ice-Cream.html) |\n| Undated case study | ROKAE cobots in **unmanned catering services** | Staff-free food service | [rokae.com](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/1088/ROKAE-Cobots-in-Unmanned-Catering-Services.html) |\n| Undated case studies | Tomato and grape **harvesting robots**, AI massage robot, moxibustion robot (all filed under \"Commercial Services\") | Arms working in unstructured, public or outdoor-adjacent settings via integrators | [Commercial Services cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/208/Commercial-Services.html) |\n| Undated case study | **CR35** in large-scale warehouse picking and sorting | Logistics handling (fixed arm) | [CR page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html) |\n| Oct 2026 | **Five Helios** wheeled humanoids at Schaeffler's Taicang plant: handling, assembly, inspection | Proves Helios mobility + manipulation in production — indoors, in a factory | [TechNode Global / PR Newswire](https://technode.global/?p=996114) |\n\n- Helios' product page lists \"Commercial Services\" as a target sector, but no public commercial-service Helios deployment was found.\n- **Nothing found** for: last-mile or outdoor delivery, street or public-space cleaning, waste collection, guide / wayfinding robots, municipal or tourism services, any Swiss or alpine deployment.\n- Research limit: web search was unavailable for this track pass; findings come from ROKAE's own site, docs and GitHub plus the company profile. Third-party news on service pilots may exist and was not checked.\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Embodied-AI line as third business pillar (2026):** Helios, Human.X, AR arms, HSA joints, CMR. Messaging centres on industrial use and on supplying force-controlled arms to other robot makers (80+ partners).\n- **iNex embodied-AI control system** 🧪 and a VLA \"intelligent core\" for Human.X 🔭 — named in marketing; no public developer access.\n- **Inference (not announced):** with \"Commercial Services\" listed as a Helios target sector and autonomous recharging as an option, service deployments outside factories are a plausible next step. No public roadmap for delivery, cleaning or outdoor robots.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What it gives you | Access |\n|---|---|---|\n| [xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP) | C++ API for all series; Ubuntu 18.04 / 20.04 / 22.04 (x86_64 and **aarch64**) and Windows; **real-time up to 1 kHz**: joint / Cartesian position, joint / Cartesian impedance, direct torque (cobots); examples in `example/` | ✅ Apache-2.0, v0.7.1 |\n| [xCoreSDK-Python](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python) | Python bindings for all series — fastest route to a scripted demo; whether real-time mode is exposed in Python is ⚠️ unverified | ✅ public |\n| [rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2) | ROS 2 **Humble** / Ubuntu 22.04; ros2_control hardware plugin, URDF + meshes, Gazebo helpers, **MoveIt 2 configs** for AR5L/R, CR7/12/18/20/35, ER3/7, Pro3/7, SR3/4/5 | ✅ Apache-2.0, stack v0.0.4 |\n| xCoreSDK-CSharp / -Android, xCorePlugin-client / -controller | C# and Android SDKs; controller / HMI plugin kits | ✅ public |\n| [docs.rokae.com](https://docs.rokae.com/en/) | SDK manuals (0.7.x, 0.7.x-AR, 0.8.x), real-time troubleshooting, ROS 2 manual | ✅ public |\n| Pendant drag teaching + path replay | Record a service motion by hand in minutes — the best fallback when planning fails | ✅ on robot |\n| Navigation stack, fleet API, VLA models, datasets, MuJoCo / Isaac assets | — | not public / not found |\n\nThe `RokaeRobot` GitHub organisation links to rokae.com and carries ROKAE (Beijing) copyright notices; treat it as official, but confirm with the engineers on site which SDK version matches the controller firmware they brought.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **Mobility:** no outdoor platform. Helios and CMR are indoor, flat-floor machines (no slope, threshold or IP data published); Helios weighs 160 kg, the smallest CMR chassis 150 kg.\n- **Not alpine-ready:** SR / CR arms are rated from 0 °C; Helios 0–45 °C non-condensing. Snow or sub-zero outdoor demos are out.\n- **No autonomy stack for fixed arms:** perception, task planning and any language / VLA layer are bring-your-own. The cobot controller has no GPU — add a PC or an edge board.\n- **Accessories not confirmed:** gripper, camera, mounting plate, 48 V supply for mobile mounting.\n- **Mounting an SR on another vendor's base** is real integration work (mechanical plate, 48 VDC power budget, e-stop chain, frame calibration) — budget half a day minimum, and get both vendors' approval.\n- **Helios / CMR software** is documented only at headline level publicly; expect to depend on on-site engineers.\n- **Public-space safety:** force limiting helps, but a kiosk demo around visitors still needs speed limits, a clear zone and an e-stop.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE Robotics profile (internal)](../ROKAE.md)\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Commercial Services application cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/208/Commercial-Services.html)\n- [ROKAE — application cases index](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/)\n- [ROKAE documentation hub](https://docs.rokae.com/en/) · [SDK docs](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK) · [ROS 2 manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot organisation](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — 10,000th humanoid force-controlled arm, Schaeffler Helios deployment, Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n","ROKAE-T01-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, playbook]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T01 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n> **Before you pick a concept:** ask what ROKAE has on site (⚠️ unverified). Concepts 1–3 need only one small xMate cobot (SR3 / SR5 / CR7). Concept 4 needs a Helios.\n> **Common stack facts:** `xCoreSDK` (C++ / Python / C#, Apache-2.0), real-time C++ up to **1 kHz** with Cartesian impedance; `rokae_ros2` on ROS 2 **Humble** / Ubuntu 22.04 with MoveIt 2 configs; controller must run xCore **v3.2.1+**. The cobot controller has no GPU — vision and planning run on your laptop or an edge board.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Unattended resort service kiosk (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** small resorts cannot staff every desk around the clock. A robot kiosk hands out pre-ordered items (rental-locker key, bath towel pack, lift pass, pharmacy pick-up, hot drink) at any hour → longer service hours without extra staff. Builds directly on ROKAE's coffee / unmanned-catering cases.\n- **Hardware:** xMate **SR5** (or SR3 / CR7) on a table · gripper (ask ROKAE; else Flexiv Grav or a simple parallel gripper) · RGB-D or USB camera · laptop. Optional: D-Robotics RDK X5 for on-device detection, XREAL glasses as the operator view.\n- **Architecture / stack:** phone or QR order → small web app → task queue → camera detects item slot and guest hand-over zone (fiducials or a light detector) → MoveIt 2 (`rokae_ros2`) or taught waypoints via Python SDK → **Cartesian impedance hand-over** (release when the guest pulls, sensed as external force). Compute on laptop.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** arm powered, SDK hello-world, gripper I/O, drag-teach one pick-and-hand-over path.\n  - **H6–24:** order app, shelf with 4–6 slots, vision for slot occupancy, parametrised picks.\n  - **H24–40:** force-based hand-over, speed limits and safety zone, error recovery (empty slot, dropped item), multilingual prompts (DE / FR / EN).\n  - **H40–48:** 20 uninterrupted cycles, video backup, pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** judge scans QR and orders \"towel + locker key\" → arm picks, presents, releases on a gentle pull → second judge blocks the arm by hand; arm yields and resumes → show the dashboard: orders served, cycle time, items left.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no gripper supplied → 3D-print a hook / tray tool; vision flaky → fixed slots with fiducials; MoveIt setup slow → pure taught waypoints.\n- **Judging angles:** *innovation* low-to-medium · *technical depth* in force-based hand-over · *impact* clear staffing story · *Leukerbad* off-hours spa / hotel / rental service.\n\n### Concept 2 — Robotic hand-off dock for delivery robots (cross-vendor)\n\n- **Problem → value:** delivery robots still need a human to load and unload them. A fixed arm at a \"micro-hub\" (hotel lobby, pharmacy, bakery) loads the right parcel into a quadruped or wheeled robot's cargo box → an end-to-end, human-free last-100-metres chain.\n- **Hardware:** xMate **SR5 / CR7** (hub arm) + a delivery carrier from another vendor on site (Unitree Go2 / B2, AGIBOT D1, Genisom M1 / L1, Magiclab MagicDog) with a cargo box + camera at the hub. Optional: SKYLANDX MetaCam map of the venue for the carrier's route.\n- **Architecture / stack:** order service assigns parcel + carrier → carrier navigates (its own stack) and parks at the dock → hub camera localises the cargo box (fiducial on the box, 6-DoF pose) → MoveIt 2 plans pick-from-shelf and place-in-box → **impedance-controlled insertion** tolerates centimetre-level parking error → \"loaded\" message releases the carrier. ROS 2 Humble topics or plain MQTT / HTTP between systems.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** split team (arm / carrier / orchestration); arm picks from a shelf; carrier drives A→B by teleop.\n  - **H6–24:** fiducial box localisation, compliant place; carrier waypoint navigation; message contract agreed and mocked.\n  - **H24–40:** full loop with 3 parcels and 2 destinations; handle mis-parking (re-detect, or ask the carrier to re-dock).\n  - **H40–48:** reliability runs, timing metrics, film a clean run.\n- **3-minute demo script:** tablet order \"pharmacy bag to Room 2\" → quadruped trots to the dock → arm finds the box, loads the bag, taps the lid closed → quadruped delivers across the hall → show the log: zero human touches.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** carrier navigation unreliable → teleoperate the drive and keep the dock autonomous (the ROKAE part is the novel bit); box pose noisy → mechanical docking guide; two-vendor networking → single laptop as the bridge.\n- **Judging angles:** *innovation* medium-high (closes a gap most delivery demos skip) · *technical depth* multi-robot handshake + compliant placement · *impact* real last-mile bottleneck · *Leukerbad* steep, car-light village where legged carriers make sense and the hubs are hotels and clinics.\n\n### Concept 3 — Contact-rich facility upkeep cell (wipe, press, turn)\n\n- **Problem → value:** public touchpoints (handrails, lift buttons, door handles, ticket machines, spa lockers) need frequent cleaning and operation; most service robots avoid contact. A force-controlled arm wipes curved surfaces with constant pressure and operates buttons / handles built for humans → hygiene and maintenance automation.\n- **Hardware:** xMate **CR7** (IP67 arm, good for wet work) or SR5 · wiping tool with sponge on the flange · RGB-D camera · mock-up panel (handrail section, lift button panel, lever handle). Mobility optional: push-cart mount, a CMR if present, or another vendor's wheeled base.\n- **Architecture / stack:** RGB-D point cloud → surface segmentation and coverage path → **real-time C++ Cartesian impedance** (low stiffness along the surface normal, target contact force ~5–10 N) at up to 1 kHz → coverage and force log as a \"cleaning certificate\". Button pressing: detect the button, approach, stop on force threshold.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** real-time mode running; constant-force contact on a flat board; safety limits.\n  - **H6–24:** point cloud → path on a curved rail; button detection and force-terminated press.\n  - **H24–40:** lever-handle turn with compliance; coverage visualisation; UV-marker or dry-erase \"dirt\" for a visible before / after.\n  - **H40–48:** tune, repeat, record.\n- **3-minute demo script:** judge scribbles on the handrail → robot scans, wipes it clean with constant pressure → presses a lift button and turns a handle → screen shows the force trace and coverage map → judge pushes the arm mid-wipe; it complies, then continues.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** real-time networking issues (the docs have a troubleshooting section — use wired Ethernet, a dedicated NIC) → fall back to the SDK's non-real-time force-control commands; perception path weak → drag-teach the path and keep force control live.\n- **Judging angles:** *innovation* medium · *technical depth* highest of the four (plays to ROKAE's core strength) · *impact* hygiene in clinics and spas · *Leukerbad* thermal baths and rehab clinics with heavy touch-surface traffic.\n\n### Concept 4 — Helios hotel concierge and runner (most ambitious; only if Helios is on site)\n\n- **Problem → value:** a mobile dual-arm robot fetches and delivers inside a hotel or clinic (luggage tag, towel, water, forgotten medication), opening doors and using the lift itself → one robot covers delivery plus light upkeep.\n- **Hardware:** **Helios** (2 × 7-DoF arms at 5 kg, omni base 1.5 m/s, 2 × 3D LiDAR, head stereo camera, Orin / Thor compute, ~4 h runtime) with grippers. Optional: XREAL glasses for staff tasking; a second vendor's VLA only if ROKAE's interface allows external arm commands.\n- **Architecture / stack:** speech or app request → LLM task planner (cloud or laptop) → skill library (navigate-to, pick, place, hand-over, press-button) → Helios SLAM navigation + arm control through its **ROS 2 / Python SDK** → on-board Jetson for perception. Interface details are not public — the first hours are discovery with ROKAE engineers.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** get API access, map the hall, drive point-to-point, move one arm from code. **Go / no-go at H6:** if navigation or arm control is not scriptable, drop to Concept 1–3.\n  - **H6–24:** pick from a table, hand-over skill, speech / app front end.\n  - **H24–40:** chain a 3-step errand; add a lift-button or door-push skill; recovery behaviours.\n  - **H40–48:** rehearse one errand until it works 4 of 5 times.\n- **3-minute demo script:** \"Helios, bring a towel to the guest at table 3\" → drives to the shelf, picks, navigates around a person, hands over compliantly → on the way back presses a (mock) lift button → dashboard shows the plan and executed skills.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** undocumented API, 160 kg robot in a crowd (keep speed low, marshal the route), limited robot time shared between teams → pre-record a full run early; reduce to navigate + hand-over only.\n- **Judging angles:** *innovation* medium (common concept, rarely shown working) · *technical depth* high if autonomous · *impact* strong hospitality / care staffing story · *Leukerbad* hotels and rehab clinics with elderly guests.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROKAE SR / CR + **Unitree / AGIBOT / Genisom / Magiclab quadruped** (separate robots) | Hub-and-carrier delivery (Concept 2) | **Low–medium** — message-level only |\n| ROKAE SR + **D-Robotics RDK X5 / S100** | On-device detection next to the arm; no laptop in the kiosk | **Low** — SDK supports aarch64 Linux; check Ubuntu version match |\n| ROKAE SR / AR + **WUJI Hand** | Dexterous operation of human interfaces (handles, switches) | **Medium** — flange adapter, power, separate hand SDK |\n| ROKAE SR + **Flexiv Grav gripper** | Reliable grasping if ROKAE brings no gripper | **Low–medium** — mechanical adapter + I/O |\n| ROKAE arm + **SKYLANDX MetaCam** | Fast 3D scan of the venue or of a surface to clean | **Low** — offline map / point cloud hand-off |\n| ROKAE arm + **XREAL glasses** | AR operator view, remote supervision of an unattended kiosk | **Medium** — custom app |\n| ROKAE **SR3** mounted on another vendor's wheeled base (Galaxea, coprod, AGIBOT) | A true mobile manipulator | **High** — mechanics, 48 VDC power, e-stop, calibration; needs both vendors' consent |\n| ROKAE arm + **Galbot / Spirit AI / Galaxea VLA** | Learned grasping policy on a ROKAE arm | **High** — models are embodiment-specific; not a 48-hour bet |\n\n## Questions to ask the ROKAE Robotics engineers on site\n\n1. Which robots, controllers and **xCore firmware version** are here — and which SDK version (0.7.x, 0.7.x-AR, 0.8.x) matches them?\n2. Is a **gripper, camera and flange adapter** included? Which gripper protocol (digital I/O, RS485)?\n3. Is **real-time mode** (1 kHz, Cartesian impedance / torque) enabled on these units, and what network setup do you recommend?\n4. Does the **Python SDK** expose real-time or force-control commands, or is that C++ only?\n5. Is a Helios or CMR on site? If so: what is the **navigation and arm API** (ROS 2 topics / actions, Python calls), and can we send our own goals?\n6. Can an SR arm run from a **48 VDC battery** on another vendor's base, and what is the peak current?\n7. Which **safety settings** (collision sensitivity, speed and force limits, virtual walls) can we change ourselves for a demo with visitors nearby?\n8. Is there any **iNex / VLA model or teleoperation data tool** we can try this weekend?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick ROKAE Robotics for T01 if** your idea has a contact-rich manipulation step at its core (hand-over, loading, wiping, pressing, opening) and you are happy with a fixed station or with pairing it with another vendor's mobile robot — or a Helios is on site and its API is confirmed open by hour 6.\n- **Avoid if** your concept is mainly outdoor navigation, street delivery, snow / terrain mobility or fleet routing, or if you need a ready-made service robot or pretrained policy out of the box.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE Robotics profile (internal)](../ROKAE.md)\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Commercial Services application cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/208/Commercial-Services.html)\n- [ROKAE SDK docs](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot organisation](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — Schaeffler Helios deployment, Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n","ROKAE-T02-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T02\nchallenge_title: \"Resilient & Safe City\"\nfit: 2\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, brief]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T02 Resilient & Safe City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 2/5** — Supporting role only: a very good force-controlled \"remote hand\", but nothing from ROKAE moves over terrain, senses hazards or has a public track record in emergency or infrastructure work.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | Fixed-base **xMate SR3 / SR5** or **CR7** (arm body **IP67**) as a teleoperated contact arm; **AR5** (7-DoF, **10 kg**, 48 V DC) if another vendor's mobile base carries it; **Helios** only if one is on site (indoor floors) |\n| Strongest proof point | None in this domain. Nearest: **five Helios at Schaeffler Taicang** doing handling, assembly and inspection (factory, Oct 2026); a teleoperated ultrasound robot on xMate ER7 Pro (undated integrator case) shows remote force-controlled contact |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | \"Remote hands for a cut-off plant room\": operator teleoperates the arm to turn a valve, flip a breaker and press a stop button on a mock utility panel, with Cartesian impedance capping contact force |\n| Biggest risk | On-site hardware is unconfirmed; arms are rated **0–50 °C**, controller cabinet **IP54**, gripper and camera not confirmed — the arm cannot reach a hazard on its own |\n| Pairs well with | Unitree / AGIBOT D1 / Genisom quadrupeds (mobility) · SKYLANDX MetaCam (3D map) · XREAL (operator view) · WUJI Hand or Flexiv Grav gripper (end-effector) · D-Robotics RDK (edge perception) |\n\n## Why\n- **Force control is the real asset:** adjustable Cartesian stiffness (SR 0–3000 N/m, CR up to 18000 N/m) and CR flange force accuracy of **0.5 N** make contact with unknown valves, doors and surfaces safe.\n- **Open developer stack:** xCore SDK on GitHub (C++ / Python / C#, Apache-2.0) with real-time control up to **1 kHz** including impedance and torque modes; `rokae_ros2` for ROS 2 Humble with MoveIt 2 and Gazebo.\n- **No T02 product:** no outdoor mobility, no hazard sensors, no rescue or infrastructure-inspection reference on ROKAE's site.\n- **Mobility is factory-grade only:** Helios (**160 kg**, IP not listed) and CMR AMRs are for flat indoor floors.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Outdoor, snow, slope or rubble scenarios; search-and-rescue mobility.\n- Wide-area monitoring, patrol or mapping (no ROKAE sensing or autonomy stack for it).\n- Teams that want a ready-made emergency-response application to extend.\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T02-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/T02-0-Track-Overview.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR series](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — 10,000th AR arm, Schaeffler deployment, Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n- [ROKAE Japan — teleoperated ultrasound robot case (ER7 Pro)](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1189/%E9%81%A0%E9%9A%94%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C%E5%9E%8B%E8%B6%85%E9%9F%B3%E6%B3%A2%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%83%BB%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html)\n","ROKAE-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, products]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T02 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T02-3-Playbook.md)\n\n> **Bottom line:** ROKAE has no resilience or public-safety product. For T02 it supplies one thing well: a force-controlled arm you can teleoperate or script to touch infrastructure safely (valves, switches, surfaces, probes). Mobility, hazard sensing and mapping must come from other vendors. What ROKAE actually brings on site is **not confirmed** — check the vendor table first.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)\n\n![xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)](../assets/rokae-xmate-cr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** the most rugged ROKAE arm body — fixed-base \"remote hands\" or contact-inspection arm in a plant room, tunnel niche mock-up or test bench. CR7 / CR12 are the realistic hackathon sizes.\n\n| Spec | CR7 | CR12 | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **7 kg** | **12 kg** | Range across family: 5–45 kg |\n| Reach | **988 mm** | **1434 mm** | |\n| Arm weight | ~25 kg | ~41 kg | |\n| Arm IP rating | **IP67** | **IP67** | CR5 is IP54 |\n| Cartesian stiffness (adjustable) | 0–6000 N/m | 0–18000 N/m | |\n| Flange force / torque sensing accuracy | **0.5 N / 0.1 Nm** (relative) | same | Collision detection via torque sensing |\n| Operating temperature | **0–50 °C** | 0–50 °C | Below-zero outdoor use is outside spec |\n| Controller (MCC) | **IP54**, ~16.6 kg, 90–264 V AC | same | Arm runs on 48 V DC |\n| Safety | TÜV-certified safety controller, PL d Cat. 3 (ISO 13849-1) per product page | | |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · **Price:** not public · **On site:** not confirmed.\n- **Developer control:** xCore SDK (C++ / Python / C#); real-time mode up to 1 kHz with joint / Cartesian position, joint / Cartesian impedance and direct torque for collaborative models (per the SDK README); ROS 2 Humble + MoveIt 2 configs exist for CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35.\n\n### xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)\n\n![xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)](../assets/rokae-xmate-sr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** the most likely arm on site and the lightest to set up — table-top teleoperation or contact-probing demo.\n\n| Spec | SR3 | SR5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** |\n| Reach | **705 mm** | **919 mm** |\n| Arm weight | ~13.8 kg | ~16.5 kg |\n| IP rating | IP54 | IP54 |\n| Cartesian stiffness (adjustable) | 0–3000 N/m | 0–3000 N/m |\n| Power | 48 V DC; 1.2 kg MSC controller | same |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · **Price:** not public · **On site:** best guess, not confirmed.\n- **Developer control:** same SDK and real-time modes as CR; ROS 2 MoveIt configs for SR3, SR4, SR5.\n\n### AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)\n\n![AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)](../assets/rokae-ar.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** light 7-DoF torque-sensing arm designed to be mounted on other robots — the only ROKAE arm that is plausible on a third-party mobile base (quadruped or wheeled) for a \"go there and operate it\" responder.\n\n| Spec | AR3 | AR5 | AR10 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** | **10 kg** |\n| Reach | 650 mm | 683 mm | 815 mm |\n| Arm weight | **8 kg** | **10 kg** | 19.5 kg |\n| DoF | 7 | 7 | 7 |\n| IP rating | IP54 | IP54 | IP54 |\n| Force resolution | ≤0.05 N (joint torque sensors standard, sampled ≥5 kHz) | same | same |\n| Power / bus | 48 V DC ±15 % · EtherCAT, 1 ms cycle | same | same |\n| Shock / temperature | 10 g impact rating · 0–50 °C | same | same |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping (10,000th unit delivered, Sep–Oct 2026) · **Price:** not public · **On site:** plausible, not confirmed.\n- **Developer control:** dedicated SDK branch 0.7.x-AR; ROS 2 MoveIt configs for AR5 left / right. Controller model, mass and power draw for a mobile mount: not public — ask on site.\n\n### Helios (Helios II)\n\n![Helios (Helios II)](../assets/rokae-helios.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** the only ROKAE platform with mobility plus two arms — an indoor responder for flat floors (plant room, corridor, station hall). Not an outdoor or rough-terrain machine.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Arms | 2 × 7-DoF, **5 kg** per arm, whole-body force control (arms, waist, neck) |\n| DoF | 23 in spec table (intro text says 42 — inconsistent on the official page) |\n| Chassis | Omnidirectional, 4 drive wheels, **1.5 m/s**, docking ±10 mm |\n| Size / weight | 750 × 650 × 1493–2000 mm · **160 kg** |\n| Runtime | **~4 h** (2 × 18 Ah), ~2 h charge 10–90 %, battery swap and auto-recharge supported |\n| Sensors | 2 × 3D LiDAR, head stereo camera, IMU; optional fisheye, chest and wrist cameras |\n| Compute | Jetson Orin NX 8 GB, AGX Orin 64 GB or Thor T5000 |\n| Comms / safety | Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, optional **5G**; optional wireless e-stop |\n| Teleoperation | Optional gamepad only (no VR / exoskeleton listed) |\n| Environment | 0–45 °C; **IP rating, slope and obstacle limits not listed** |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping (five units at Schaeffler Taicang, Oct 2026) · **Price:** not public · **On site:** possible, heavy to ship.\n- **Developer control:** ROS 2 and a Python SDK for secondary development; navigation-stack details not public.\n\n### CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)\n\n![CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)](../assets/rokae-cmr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** conceptually an unattended \"rounds\" robot for technical rooms (read gauges, press buttons), but it is a factory AMR and unlikely to be on site.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Arm payload | 7–45 kg depending on model (CMR-S carries SR, CR7 or CR12) |\n| Speed / navigation | 1.3–1.5 m/s · laser SLAM, 2 LiDARs · positioning ±5 to ±10 mm |\n| Runtime | 7–12 h |\n| Chassis weight | 150–1500 kg; CMR-S fits aisles down to 800 mm |\n| IP / slope / obstacle | not listed |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · **Price:** not public · **Developer control:** CMR-specific APIs not public.\n\n### xCore / iNex\n\n![xCore / iNex](../assets/rokae-xcore.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** the software layer you will actually build on — see \"Software, models and data\" below. iNex (embodied-AI control system) is 🧪 with no public developer access.\n\n### Lower relevance\n- **HSA joint modules** — only useful if you build a custom mechanism (for example a valve-turning tool); too slow a path for 48 hours.\n- **Human.X** 🧪, **NB / XB industrial arms**, **welding package**, **xMate ZR**, **xMate ER Pro** (⚠️ unverified status) — no specific T02 role.\n\n## What ROKAE Robotics already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing specific to emergency response, hazard monitoring, security patrol or infrastructure inspection was found** on ROKAE's official site, docs or GitHub. The closest adjacent evidence:\n\n| Date | What | Relevance to T02 | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Oct 2026 | Five Helios robots at Schaeffler's Taicang factory for parts handling, assembly and **inspection** | Proves unattended mobile manipulation indoors; it is factory quality work, not infrastructure | [TechNode Global / PR Newswire](https://technode.global/?p=996114) |\n| Undated case study | Teleoperated ultrasound diagnosis robot on xMate ER7 Pro | Proves remote operation with force-controlled contact — the same pattern as remote intervention | [ROKAE Japan case](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1189/%E9%81%A0%E9%9A%94%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C%E5%9E%8B%E8%B6%85%E9%9F%B3%E6%B3%A2%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%83%BB%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html) |\n| Undated (product page) | AI and 3D vision inspection of automotive seats and hot-formed parts with CR arms | Arm-held camera inspection, factory context | [xMate CR page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html) |\n| Undated (case list) | Chemical catalyst preparation with a cobot; \"electric power\" named as a general-industry scenario without a case | Hint of lab / utility use, no details public | [General Industry cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/343/General-Industry.html) |\n\nResearch note: general web search was unavailable during this run, so third-party integrator projects (for example power-utility or explosion-proof cobot applications built on ROKAE arms) could not be checked — ⚠️ unverified whether any exist. Ask the ROKAE engineers.\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced:** nothing aimed at resilience or public safety. 2026 messaging is about being a force-control arm and joint supplier to humanoid makers (10,000 AR arms, 80+ partners) and industrial alliances (Northeast Intelligent Manufacturing Alliance, Nov 2026).\n- **Inference:** Helios options — 5G, wireless e-stop, autonomous recharging, battery swap — are the building blocks of unattended remote operation, which could later extend to utility or plant-room rounds. This is our reading, not a ROKAE statement.\n- **Inference:** iNex 🧪 and the VLA \"intelligent core\" 🔭 mentioned for Human.X could give higher-level autonomy; no public access today.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What you get | T02 use | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP) | Apache-2.0; Ubuntu 18.04 / 20.04 / 22.04 (x86_64, aarch64) and Windows; needs xCore controller **v3.2.1+**; non-real-time moves (MoveJ / MoveL / MoveC), force-control commands, IO, RS-485 tool comms, drag and path replay, collision detection; **real-time mode up to 1 kHz** with joint / Cartesian position, joint / Cartesian impedance and direct torque on collaborative arms | Teleoperation with compliant contact; force-guarded probing | ✅ (binaries come from the GitHub release, v0.7.1) |\n| [xCoreSDK-Python](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python) | Python interface \"for all series\" | Fast scripting of motions and force commands | ✅ — whether real-time mode is exposed in Python was not confirmed |\n| [rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2) | ROS 2 **Humble** / Ubuntu 22.04, `ros2_control` + **MoveIt 2**, URDF and meshes, Gazebo helpers, examples; configs for AR5 L/R, CR7/12/18/20/35, ER3/7, Pro3/7, SR3/4/5; stack v0.0.4 on SDK 0.7.1 | Collision-aware planning in a scanned scene; simulation before hardware time | ✅ — sim-without-robot workflow not confirmed |\n| [xCorePlugin-client / -controller](https://github.com/RokaeRobot) | Plugin kits for pendant and controller | Not needed in 48 hours | ✅ |\n| Teleoperation toolkit, datasets, pretrained models | — | — | not public — bring your own |\n\nGitHub note: the `RokaeRobot` organization is named \"ROKAE Robotics\", links to rokae.com and carries a ROKAE (Beijing) copyright; a back-link from rokae.com was not checked, so treat it as very likely official.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No mobility for the real world:** nothing from ROKAE handles stairs, snow, gravel or slopes. Helios and CMR publish no IP, slope or obstacle figures.\n- **Environment:** arms are rated **0–50 °C**; Leukerbad outdoors in November is below that. CR arm bodies are IP67 but the controller cabinet is IP54 and needs mains AC.\n- **No hazard sensing:** no gas, thermal, acoustic or structural sensors; add your own.\n- **No T02 autonomy or application software:** no patrol, mapping, anomaly detection or incident tooling.\n- **Teleoperation:** only a gamepad option on Helios is listed; you write the teleop layer yourself. Network-loss behaviour is not documented publicly.\n- **End-effectors and cameras:** not confirmed as supplied.\n- **No Swiss presence or local references** found; prices not public.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR series](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n- [ROKAE — General Industry cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/343/General-Industry.html)\n- [ROKAE official site (EN) — navigation and news](https://www.rokae.com/en/)\n- [ROKAE SDK documentation](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [ROKAE ROS 2 package manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot organization](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — 10,000th AR arm and Schaeffler deployment, 27 Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n- [ROKAE Japan — teleoperated ultrasound robot case (ER7 Pro)](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1189/%E9%81%A0%E9%9A%94%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C%E5%9E%8B%E8%B6%85%E9%9F%B3%E6%B3%A2%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%83%BB%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html)\n- [ROKAE company profile (this pack)](../ROKAE.md)\n","ROKAE-T02-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, playbook]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T02 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n> **Read first:** fit is **2/5**. ROKAE gives you a force-controlled arm and an open SDK, not a resilience system. Every concept below uses the arm as the \"hand that touches infrastructure\" and gets mobility, mapping or operator interface elsewhere. Which ROKAE units are on site is **not confirmed** — settle that in the first 30 minutes.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Remote hands for a cut-off plant room (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** when a road is closed by avalanche, rockfall or flood, nobody can reach a pump room, switch cabinet or cable-car drive room. A pre-installed arm lets an operator turn a valve, reset a breaker or press a stop button from far away, with contact force capped so nothing gets broken.\n- **Hardware:** one fixed-base **xMate SR3 / SR5 or CR7**; any gripper available (ask ROKAE; otherwise Flexiv Grav or a 3D-printed valve key / finger); USB or depth camera; mock panel with a quarter-turn valve, rocker switch, e-stop button and a gauge. Operator input: gamepad or SpaceMouse; optional XREAL glasses for the camera view.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera → operator view (plus optional gauge reading with a small vision model) → teleop node maps input to Cartesian velocity targets → **xCore SDK real-time Cartesian impedance** (C++, up to 1 kHz) on a Linux PC wired to the controller. Force estimates from the arm drive a contact-force bar in the UI. Simple path if real-time mode is unavailable: non-real-time `MoveL` jog steps plus SDK force-control commands from Python.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** confirm arm, controller version (**v3.2.1+** needed for SDK 0.7.1), gripper; run SDK example; jog from code; build the mock panel.\n  - **H6–24:** teleop loop with impedance; camera stream; force display; pre-taught \"macros\" (approach valve, approach switch) recorded by drag teaching.\n  - **H24–40:** latency and network-drop handling (watchdog → hold position); gauge reading; operator UI; rehearse.\n  - **H40–48:** freeze code, record backup video, polish pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) \"The road is closed, the pump room is flooding.\" (0:30) Operator, not looking at the arm, reads the gauge through the camera. (1:00) Triggers the \"approach valve\" macro, then closes the valve by hand control; force bar stays under the limit. (1:45) Flips the breaker, presses the stop button. (2:15) Pull the network cable: arm holds safely, resumes on reconnect. (2:40) Cost and deployment story.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no real-time licence or mode → non-real-time jog. No gripper → printed tool fixed to the flange. Laggy video → macros do the approach, human only confirms.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* modest; *technical depth* in impedance teleop and fail-safe behaviour; *impact* clear for utilities; *Leukerbad relevance* strong — a mountain village with a single access road and thermal-water and cable-car plant rooms.\n\n### Concept 2 — Force-controlled tap-test inspector\n\n- **Problem → value:** hammer sounding (tapping and listening for hollow sounds) is still a standard manual check for concrete linings and bonded surfaces. A force-controlled arm can tap with repeatable energy and log results as a map, making inspections comparable over time.\n- **Hardware:** **xMate SR5 or CR7 / CR12**; a small tapper (solenoid or spring hammer) and a contact microphone on the flange; test wall made of tiles or concrete slabs with hidden voids. Optional: SKYLANDX MetaCam scan of the test wall; D-Robotics RDK for on-edge audio classification.\n- **Architecture / stack:** scan or hand-taught surface → grid of tap points → MoveIt 2 (`rokae_ros2`, Humble) or SDK `MoveL` for approach → impedance / force-guarded contact to find the surface → tap → audio features (spectral centroid, decay) → simple classifier (solid vs hollow) → heat map overlaid on a photo or point cloud. Compute on a laptop.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** arm motion from code; build the tapper and test wall; record first audio.\n  - **H6–24:** force-guarded touch-and-tap routine; collect labelled taps; train classifier.\n  - **H24–40:** automatic grid scan; heat map; repeatability check; optional 3D overlay.\n  - **H40–48:** blind test on a panel the team did not label; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) Show a wall that looks intact. (0:30) Arm scans a 5 × 5 grid, tapping gently. (1:45) Heat map appears, red zone marked. (2:15) Open the panel to reveal the void. (2:40) Where it goes next: gallery, tunnel and retaining-wall checks from a vehicle or a mobile base.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** audio too noisy in the hall → use an accelerometer on the tapper or reduce to two clearly different materials. No scan → hand-taught plane. Classifier weak → show raw spectrograms side by side.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* good (contact sensing, not another camera demo); *technical depth* in force control plus signal processing; *impact* in ageing-infrastructure maintenance; *Leukerbad relevance* via road galleries, tunnels and retaining structures on alpine access routes.\n\n### Concept 3 — Mobile first responder: go there and operate it\n\n- **Problem → value:** after an incident, someone must enter a possibly unsafe building to shut off gas or water or open a door. A mobile base with a compliant arm does the first entry.\n- **Hardware:** option A — **Helios** if on site (indoor, flat floors). Option B — **AR5** (10 kg, 48 V DC) or **SR3** mounted on another vendor's base (Unitree B2 / Go2, AGIBOT D1, Genisom M1 / L1, or a wheeled base). SKYLANDX MetaCam for the map; XREAL for the operator.\n- **Architecture / stack:** base navigation from the base vendor's stack (or Helios ROS 2) → operator selects target → base parks → arm under impedance teleop or a scripted valve-turn → status back to operator. Compute: base computer for navigation, laptop or Jetson for arm control and video.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** go / no-go on mounting: payload, 48 V supply, controller size, e-stop chain. If no-go by H6, switch to Concept 1.\n  - **H6–24:** mechanical mount and power; arm control from the base computer; base teleop.\n  - **H24–40:** combined mission in a mock corridor: drive, park, operate valve; map display.\n  - **H40–48:** rehearse the single mission; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) Alarm: gas smell in the utility corridor. (0:30) Robot drives in; map and video build up. (1:30) Parks at the valve; arm closes it with force shown. (2:20) Drives out; incident log displayed.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** mounting or power not feasible in time (highest risk); Helios absent; two SDKs to integrate. Fallback: base and arm as two cooperating robots — base scouts and maps, fixed arm operates the panel.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* and *impact* high; *technical depth* in cross-vendor integration; *Leukerbad relevance* for hotel, spa and clinic technical areas during an evacuation.\n\n### Concept 4 — Click-to-act in a digital twin (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** teleoperating every centimetre is slow and needs a skilled operator. Let the operator click a valve or switch in a 3D twin; the robot plans and executes the contact itself and asks for help only when force readings look wrong.\n- **Hardware:** xMate SR / CR arm (or AR on a base); depth camera; SKYLANDX MetaCam scan; XREAL glasses or a laptop viewer.\n- **Architecture / stack:** scan → point cloud registered to the robot frame → operator click → open-vocabulary detector or VLM labels the object and chooses a skill (turn, push, pull) → MoveIt 2 plans a collision-free approach using the scan → skill runs under Cartesian impedance with force thresholds → anomaly (force too high, no motion) hands control back to manual teleop from Concept 1.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** Concept 1 basics working; scan the mock panel; camera-to-robot calibration.\n  - **H6–24:** three hand-written skills with force guards; MoveIt planning scene from the scan.\n  - **H24–40:** click interface; detector / VLM for skill choice; hand-back to manual.\n  - **H40–48:** limit to the two most reliable skills; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) Show the twin of the plant room. (0:30) Click the valve → robot closes it. (1:15) Click the breaker → robot flips it. (1:45) Jammed valve: force limit reached, system asks the operator, who takes over. (2:30) Scaling story: one operator supervising many sites.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** calibration errors, planning failures, VLM latency. Fallback: fixed, pre-registered targets with the same UI; drop the VLM and use buttons.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* and *technical depth* highest; *impact* in operator productivity; *Leukerbad relevance* same as Concept 1 with a stronger \"one operator for the whole valley\" story.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROKAE arm (fixed) + XREAL glasses | Head-up camera view and status for the remote operator | Low — video stream and overlay only |\n| ROKAE arm + SKYLANDX MetaCam | 3D scan as MoveIt planning scene and as the twin for click-to-act or heat maps | Medium — one-off registration of scan to robot frame |\n| ROKAE arm + WUJI Hand or Flexiv Grav gripper | Real grasping of handles, plugs and tools | Medium — mechanical adapter, separate hand SDK |\n| ROKAE AR5 / SR3 on Unitree, AGIBOT D1 or Genisom quadruped | Mobile manipulation beyond flat floors | High — mount, 48 V power, controller placement, e-stop, two SDKs |\n| Quadruped scout (any vendor) + fixed ROKAE arm | Scout maps and finds the fault; arm acts at the panel — no mechanical coupling | Low to medium — shared map and messaging only |\n| ROKAE arm + D-Robotics RDK X5 / S100 | On-edge gauge reading, audio or vision classification near the arm | Low to medium |\n| ROKAE arm + OriginFlow EMG capture | Operator arm motion or gesture as teleop input | Medium to high — mapping and filtering, novelty value |\n| Helios + Binabik AI or coprod stack | Higher-level task agent on a mobile dual-arm robot | High — depends on what each party exposes on site |\n\n## Questions to ask the ROKAE Robotics engineers on site\n\n1. Which models are here, with which controller and xCore version (is it **v3.2.1 or later**), and how many units can one team book?\n2. Is real-time mode enabled on these units? Which modes work (Cartesian impedance, direct torque), and is it reachable from Python or only C++?\n3. What grippers, tool flanges, tool IO (RS-485) and camera mounts are available to borrow?\n4. How do we read estimated external force / torque at the tool through the SDK, at what rate, and can collision thresholds be set from code?\n5. What happens when the control PC or network drops during real-time control — hold, brake, error state — and how do we recover from code?\n6. For an AR arm on a third-party base: which controller is needed, its size, mass, power draw and whether it runs from a 48 V battery?\n7. If Helios is here: what is exposed over ROS 2 (navigation goals, map, arm control, teleop), and what are its slope and threshold limits?\n8. Does `rokae_ros2` run the Gazebo simulation without a robot, and is there a URDF for the exact models on site?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick ROKAE Robotics for T02 if** your idea is about *touching* infrastructure — remote valve or switch operation, contact inspection, force-guarded intervention — and you are happy with a fixed-base arm or have a confirmed mobile base to pair it with.\n- **Avoid if** your idea needs to move through terrain, snow or rubble, patrol or map large areas, or you want a vendor with existing emergency-response references; choose a quadruped or scanning vendor as the lead and add ROKAE only as the arm.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR series](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n- [ROKAE SDK documentation](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [GitHub — RokaeRobot organization](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [ROKAE company profile (this pack)](../ROKAE.md)\n","ROKAE-T03-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T03\nchallenge_title: \"Sustainable City\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, brief]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T03 Sustainable City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — A solid force-controlled arm for sorting, disassembly or picking cells, but ROKAE has no sustainability-specific product, model or dataset; you bring the perception and the idea.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **xMate SR5** (5 kg, 919 mm, 48 VDC, 16.5 kg) or **CR7** (7 kg, 988 mm, IP67) on a table with your own camera and gripper; **AR5** 7-DoF arm if that is what is on site |\n| Strongest proof point | **CR35 parcel picking and sorting**: 13M+ real parcel-handling operations, 0.01% grasp-damage rate (ROKAE case page, listed 12 Nov 2026, customer not named). Adjacent: CR35 lithium-battery cell-module handling; tomato and grape harvesting cases (no details published) |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Camera + open-vocabulary detector + SR/CR arm sorting mixed waste (PET, cans, glass, paper) into bins, with force-controlled \"press test\" to tell rigid from soft items |\n| Biggest risk | Gripper, camera and compute are **not confirmed as supplied**; SDK real-time modes and ROS 2 details ⚠️ unverified — the first 6 hours decide everything |\n| Pairs well with | D-Robotics (RDK X5 edge AI) · WUJI (dexterous hand) · SKYLANDX (3D scan) · Unitree / Genisom quadruped (mobile litter collection) · Galbot / Galaxea (grasp models) |\n\n## Why\n- **Force control is the differentiator**: adjustable Cartesian stiffness (SR: 0–3000 N/m) helps with unknown, deformable or fragile waste and with unscrewing / prying in repair and disassembly tasks.\n- **Proven in sorting-like work at scale** (CR35 parcels; JD Logistics \"Canglang\" picking robot uses AR5 arms, 99.9% picking accuracy).\n- **Low-power, portable hardware**: 48 VDC arms, 1.2 kg controller — fits a \"low-energy automation\" story and a desk-sized demo.\n- **Documented SDK** in C++, Python and C# plus a ROS 2 package.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Outdoor, mobile or environmental-monitoring builds (snow, air, water) — arms are fixed-base; Helios / CMR are unlikely to be on site.\n- Teams that want a ready VLA or waste-sorting model — none is public from ROKAE.\n- EV-charging automation as a ROKAE-proven use case — no public ROKAE deployment found.\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T03-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T03-Sustainable-City/T03-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","ROKAE-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, products]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T03 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T03-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Bottom line:** ROKAE sells arms, not sustainability solutions. For T03 it is the manipulation half of a sorting, disassembly, picking or lab-automation cell. What is physically on site is **not confirmed** — check the vendor table before committing.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)\n\n![xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)](../assets/rokae-xmate-sr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** the most likely on-site arm and the easiest to build a desk-sized sorting, repair or plant-care cell around. Light, 48 V, tiny controller.\n\n| Spec | SR3 | SR5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** |\n| Reach | **705 mm** | **919 mm** |\n| Arm weight | ~13.8 kg | ~16.5 kg |\n| Repeatability | ±0.03 mm | ±0.03 mm |\n| Max tool speed | ≤1.5 m/s | ≤2.0 m/s |\n| Force control accuracy | 0.5 N / 0.1 Nm | 0.1 N / 0.02 Nm |\n| Adjustable stiffness | 0–3000 N/m · 0–300 Nm/rad | same |\n| Protection / temperature | IP54 · 0–50 °C | same |\n| Power | 48 VDC (consumption not public) | same |\n| Controller | MSC, 203 × 175 × 42 mm, ~1.2 kg, IP20; 5 DI / 5 DO, EtherCAT, 3× GbE, RS485 | same |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · price not public · on-site availability unconfirmed.\n- **Developer control:** xCore SDK (C++ / Python / C#) for motion commands and I/O; drag teaching on the pendant; ROS 2 package. Real-time streaming exists in the C++ SDK (docs list torque-control troubleshooting), but rate and exact modes are ⚠️ unverified.\n\n### xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)\n\n![xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)](../assets/rokae-xmate-cr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** more reach and payload plus **IP67** — the better choice for wet, dirty waste or heavier items (bottle crates, e-waste, battery modules). The CR35 is ROKAE's proven sorting arm but is far too heavy to expect on site.\n\n| Spec | CR7 | CR12 | CR35 (35 kg variant) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **7 kg** | **12 kg** | **35 kg** |\n| Reach | **988 mm** | **1434 mm** | **2246 mm** |\n| Arm weight | ~25 kg | ~41 kg | ~180 kg |\n| Repeatability | ±0.02 mm | ±0.03 mm | ±0.05 mm |\n| Max tool speed | ≤3.2 m/s | ≤3.0 m/s | not stated |\n| Protection | IP67 | IP67 | IP67 |\n| Adjustable stiffness | 0–6000 N/m | 0–18000 N/m | not listed |\n| Power | 48 VDC arm; controller on single-phase AC | same | controller 190–240 V AC |\n\n- CR5 is the exception on protection: **IP54**, 5 kg, 1406 mm.\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · price not public.\n- **Developer control:** same xCore SDK and ROS 2 path as SR; tool-flange force / torque sensing listed for CR5–CR20.\n\n### AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)\n\n![AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)](../assets/rokae-ar.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** 7-DoF arm with joint torque sensors — best for cluttered bins, reaching around obstacles, and dual-arm disassembly (one arm holds, one unscrews). Used on the JD Logistics picking robot.\n\n| Spec | AR3 | AR5 | AR10 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** | **10 kg** |\n| Reach | 650 mm | 683 mm (JD case page says 700 mm) | 815 mm |\n| Arm weight | 8 kg | 10 kg | 19.5 kg |\n| DoF | 7 | 7 | 7 |\n| Force resolution | ≤0.05 N | same | same |\n| Bus / power / protection | EtherCAT 1 ms · 48 VDC · IP54 | same | same |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping (10,000th unit delivered, Oct 2026) · price not public.\n- **Developer control:** dedicated SDK branch **0.7.x-AR**. Needs a host controller; no on-board perception or GPU.\n\n### CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)\n\n![CMR series (CMR-S, CMR-L, CMR-CR)](../assets/rokae-cmr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** resource-efficient intralogistics (one mobile arm serving several stations). Relevant as a concept reference; **unlikely on site** (chassis 150–1500 kg).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Arm payload / reach | **7–45 kg** / 988–2246 mm (CR arms) |\n| Navigation | Laser SLAM, no floor markers, 360° LiDAR coverage |\n| Positioning | ±10 mm quoted on oily floors; docking ±5 to ±10 mm by model |\n| Speed / runtime | 1.3–1.5 m/s · 7–12 h |\n| Vision | 2D / 3D vision options for final positioning |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping · price not public. Developer interface for the base: not public.\n\n### Helios (Helios II)\n\n![Helios (Helios II)](../assets/rokae-helios.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** the only ROKAE platform with mobility, two arms and on-board GPU in one unit — would enable a roaming recycling or building-service robot. **Possible but not expected on site** (160 kg).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Arms | 2× AR-class, **5 kg per arm** |\n| Base | Omnidirectional, **1.5 m/s**, indoor flat floors |\n| Runtime | ~**4 h** |\n| Sensors | 2× 3D LiDAR, head stereo camera |\n| Compute | Jetson Orin NX 8 GB / AGX Orin 64 GB / Thor T5000 options |\n| Dev access | ROS 2 + Python SDK |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ (five units at Schaeffler Taicang, Oct 2026) · price not public. DoF count is inconsistent on the product page (23 vs 42).\n\n### xCore / iNex\n\n![xCore / iNex](../assets/rokae-xcore.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** the software you actually program. xCore ✅ is the controller and SDK for all arms; iNex 🧪 is the announced embodied-AI control system with no public developer access.\n\n## What ROKAE Robotics already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing is marketed as \"sustainability\", recycling, waste sorting or EV charging.** The closest public evidence is adjacent work. All items below are ROKAE case pages with a **listing date of 12 Nov 2026** (deployment dates and most customers are not given):\n\n| Area (T03 link) | What is public | Evidence quality |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sorting at scale (→ waste / reuse sorting) | **CR35**, ceiling-mounted, combined suction-and-grip tool, warehouse parcel picking and sorting: **13M+ parcel operations**, **99.99%** system stability, **0.01%** grasp-damage rate. Customer not named. [Case](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2629/CR35-Advances-Large-Scale-Warehouse-Picking-&-Sorting.html) | Good numbers, self-reported |\n| Embodied picking (→ resource-efficient logistics) | **JD Logistics \"Canglang\"** picking robot uses **AR5** arms: **99.9%** picking accuracy, **85%+** SKU coverage, 24/7, no warehouse retrofit. [Case](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2630/Embodied-Intelligence-in-Action:-Advancing-the-Future-of-Smart-Warehousin.html) | Named customer; the robot and its AI are JD's, the arms are ROKAE's |\n| Battery / energy-storage manufacturing (→ energy transition) | CR35 handling large lithium cell modules; an automated line for liquid-cooled storage module packs (energy-storage use is inferred from the title); an NB220 lithium-battery handling case is referenced. [Cell modules](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2623/Reliable-Cell-Module-Handling-for-Battery-Manufacturing.html) · [Pack line](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2622/Liguid-Cooled-Storage-Module-Pack-Automated-Assembly-Line.html) | Titles and short text only; no numbers |\n| Agriculture (→ urban farming) | Tomato-harvesting and grape-harvesting robot cases on cobots. [Tomato](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2627/Fruit-Picking：Tomato-Harvesting-Robot.html) · [Grape](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2626/Fruit-Picking：Grape-Harvesting-Robot.html) | Title only; model, partner and performance not public |\n| Lab automation (→ green chemistry, water testing) | \"Chemical Catalyst Preparation\" cobot case. [Case](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2625/Chemical-Catalyst-Preparation.html) | Title only |\n| Shared-resource logistics | CMR mobile manipulators for machine tending and inter-station transfer. [Application page](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/624/CMR-Series-Composite-Robot.html) | Generic application page, no named customer |\n\nNot found in public ROKAE material: recycling or waste-sorting lines, battery **dis**assembly, EV-charging robots, environmental monitoring, building automation.\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **No sustainability roadmap is public.**\n- Stated direction (Oct 2026 press release): be the force-control arm and joint supplier for embodied-AI robots (AR arms, HSA joints), plus own platforms (Helios, Human.X) and the iNex control system 🧪.\n- **Inference:** the JD Logistics and CR35 parcel cases suggest logistics picking and sorting is the embodied-AI application ROKAE pushes hardest; waste or reuse sorting is technically the same problem and a plausible pitch to their engineers.\n- **Inference:** battery-manufacturing cases indicate \"new energy\" is treated as a customer industry, not as a sustainability product line.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | Status | Use in a 48-hour T03 build |\n|---|---|---|\n| xCore SDK — C++, Python, C# (0.7.x, 0.7.x-AR, 0.8.x) | ✅ on docs.rokae.com | Python for pick-and-place and I/O; C++ if you need real-time force behaviour |\n| C++ \"Scene Examples\" and real-time-mode FAQ (incl. torque control) | ✅ listed in the docs index | Starting point for compliant contact; contents not checked in detail |\n| \"Rokae xMate ROS 2 User Manual\" + package | ✅ exists; distro, MoveIt and simulation support ⚠️ unverified | Use only if the on-site engineers confirm a working distro |\n| Drag teaching, pendant flow programming, RokaeStudio | ✅ | Teach bin and drop poses in minutes; good fallback |\n| Pretrained models (VLA, grasping, waste classes) | not public | Bring your own: open-vocabulary detector + grasp heuristics, or another vendor's model |\n| Datasets, simulation assets (MuJoCo / Isaac) | not public / not found | Do not plan around simulation |\n| Official GitHub org | not confirmed | Get the SDK from the docs portal or from the engineers on a USB stick |\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No domain content**: no waste, recycling, EV-charging or environmental product, model or dataset from ROKAE.\n- **Arms only (most likely)**: no gripper, camera or GPU confirmed as supplied; no mobility unless Helios or CMR turns up.\n- **Indoor hardware**: SR and AR are IP54, 0–50 °C; CR is IP67 but still a fixed-base indoor arm. No outdoor, snow or slope capability.\n- **Energy data missing**: power consumption is not published, so \"low-energy\" claims must be measured on site (bring a plug-in power meter).\n- **Developer unknowns**: real-time rate and modes, ROS 2 distro, and simulation assets are unverified.\n- **Evidence is thin**: most relevant case pages carry only a title, a short text and a listing date; customers are mostly unnamed.\n- **Research limit**: open web search was unavailable for this document; findings rest on ROKAE's own product, case and docs pages plus the company profile. Third-party coverage (for example recycling or charging integrators using ROKAE arms) may exist and was not checked.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots product page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR product page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Mobile Manipulator CMR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/599/Mobile-Manipulator-CMR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE — application cases index](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/)\n- [ROKAE — General Industry cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/343/General-Industry.html)\n- [ROKAE — Commercial Services cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/208/Commercial-Services.html)\n- [ROKAE — CMR Series Composite Robot application page](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/624/CMR-Series-Composite-Robot.html)\n- [ROKAE case — CR35 warehouse picking and sorting](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2629/CR35-Advances-Large-Scale-Warehouse-Picking-&-Sorting.html)\n- [ROKAE case — embodied intelligence in smart warehousing (JD Logistics, AR5)](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2630/Embodied-Intelligence-in-Action:-Advancing-the-Future-of-Smart-Warehousin.html)\n- [ROKAE case — cell module handling for battery manufacturing](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2623/Reliable-Cell-Module-Handling-for-Battery-Manufacturing.html)\n- [ROKAE case — liquid-cooled storage module pack assembly line](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2622/Liguid-Cooled-Storage-Module-Pack-Automated-Assembly-Line.html)\n- [ROKAE case — tomato harvesting robot](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2627/Fruit-Picking：Tomato-Harvesting-Robot.html)\n- [ROKAE case — grape harvesting robot](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2626/Fruit-Picking：Grape-Harvesting-Robot.html)\n- [ROKAE case — chemical catalyst preparation](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2625/Chemical-Catalyst-Preparation.html)\n- [ROKAE documentation hub](https://docs.rokae.com/en/)\n- [ROKAE SDK documentation index](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [ROKAE ROS 2 package manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — 10,000th AR arm, Schaeffler Helios deployment, Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114)\n- [ROKAE Robotics company profile (this vault)](../ROKAE.md)\n","ROKAE-T03-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, playbook]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T03 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Assumptions to verify in the first hour:** which arm is on site (SR / CR / AR), whether a gripper and camera come with it, which SDK version runs on its controller, and whether a Linux PC with GPU is available. Every concept below assumes **one fixed-base ROKAE arm + your own RGB-D camera + a simple gripper or suction cup + a laptop**.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — \"Sort-by-touch\" hotel recycling cell (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Leukerbad's hotels, clinics and spa restaurants produce mixed recyclables (PET, aluminium, glass, paper) that are collected as separate fractions in Switzerland. Contamination ruins batches. A desk-sized cell sorts by vision and confirms by touch → cleaner fractions, less staff time.\n- **Hardware:** xMate **SR5** or **CR7** (CR7 preferred for IP67 if items are wet) · RGB-D camera above the tray · two-finger or suction gripper · 4 bins · optional D-Robotics RDK X5 for edge inference.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: open-vocabulary detector or a small fine-tuned classifier on the laptop / RDK → class + pick point from depth.\n  - Planning: top-down grasp, fixed drop poses taught by drag teaching.\n  - Control: xCore SDK **Python** motion commands + digital I/O for the gripper. Force step: lower until contact force threshold, squeeze / press and read the force response to separate rigid (glass, can) from soft (PET, carton) when vision is unsure.\n  - Compute: laptop; robot controller only executes moves.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: get SDK talking to the arm, jog from Python, wire gripper I/O, mount camera, hand-eye calibration.\n  - H6–24: detection + pick-and-place loop for 4 classes; teach bin poses; log successes.\n  - H24–40: add force \"press test\", contamination reject bin, dashboard (items sorted, estimated kg CO₂e avoided — state your factor and source).\n  - H40–48: tune reliability on 30 real items from the venue, record backup video, rehearse.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) show a real mixed bag from the venue kitchen → (0:30) robot sorts 6–8 items live → (1:30) hand it a tricky item (crushed can vs. foil-lined carton); robot presses, reclassifies → (2:15) dashboard: purity, items/hour, measured arm power draw → (2:40) scaling to hotels and the clinic.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no gripper supplied → 3D-print a scoop or use a vacuum cup with a hobby pump · force readout not accessible from Python → use the controller's collision / contact detection as a binary touch signal, or drop the touch step · detector weak → restrict to 3 visually distinct classes.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* — tactile confirmation, not just vision. *Technical depth* — closed loop of vision + force. *Impact* — measurable purity and energy numbers. *Leukerbad* — hospitality waste in a car-light resort where collection trips are costly.\n\n### Concept 2 — Repair-and-reuse station: compliant disassembly of small e-waste\n\n- **Problem → value:** Small electronics and rental gear (e-bike batteries, ski-rental electronics, hotel devices) are shredded rather than repaired because opening them is manual. A force-controlled arm that unscrews and pries housings open makes reuse and battery recovery cheaper → circular economy.\n- **Hardware:** **AR5** (7-DoF) or **SR5** · electric screwdriver or bit tool on the flange · fixture / vice · wrist or overhead camera · optional second arm or WUJI hand to hold the part.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: screw-head detection (small model or classical circle detection) → 3D pose from depth.\n  - Planning: per-device \"disassembly recipe\" (ordered screw list); an LLM can generate the recipe from a photo as a stretch goal.\n  - Control: approach in position mode, then compliant insertion with low Cartesian stiffness so the bit seats itself; torque / force threshold to detect seated bit and loosened screw. C++ SDK if real-time impedance is needed; Python if the non-real-time force commands suffice.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm stiffness / force-control access from the SDK with the ROKAE engineers; build the fixture; pick ONE target device with 4–6 visible screws.\n  - H6–24: screw detection + compliant seating + unscrew cycle on one screw type.\n  - H24–40: full sequence, lid removal by prying or suction, part sorting into \"reuse / recycle / hazardous (battery)\".\n  - H40–48: reliability runs, second device type if time, video backup.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) the repair-vs-shred problem with one number → (0:30) robot finds and removes screws live, show force trace on screen as the bit seats → (1:45) lid off, battery placed in the safe bin → (2:20) show the recipe file: new device = new recipe, same skills.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** screw seating unreliable → enlarge compliance, use a magnetic bit, or pre-loosen screws and say so · no screwdriver tool → demonstrate prying / snap-fit opening only · lithium cells: use **discharged or dummy cells only** and never pierce.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* — force-controlled disassembly is rare at hackathons. *Technical depth* — contact-rich control. *Impact* — right-to-repair and battery recovery. *Leukerbad* — seasonal rental equipment and e-bike fleets.\n\n### Concept 3 — Vertical-farm tender for spa-hotel kitchens\n\n- **Problem → value:** Fresh herbs and greens are trucked up to 1,400 m all winter. A compact indoor rack tended by one arm (seed, inspect, water by need, harvest) cuts transport and water use. ROKAE lists tomato- and grape-harvesting cases, so the arm class is proven for produce handling (details not public).\n- **Hardware:** **SR5** / **CR7** in front of a 2–3 shelf rack · RGB-D camera on the wrist · soft gripper or scissors tool · small pump and moisture sensor on a microcontroller · optional XREAL glasses for a grower's status overlay.\n- **Architecture / stack:** Perception: plant segmentation + ripeness / health classification per tray → Planning: per-plant action list (water X ml, harvest, skip) → Control: Python SDK moves; gentle contact via low stiffness when gripping stems · Compute: laptop or RDK X5; log water and energy per harvested gram.\n- **48-hour plan:** H0–6 rack, plants from a local shop, arm reach check · H6–24 scan routine + watering by measured need · H24–40 harvest motion with compliant grasp, resource dashboard · H40–48 polish and rehearse.\n- **3-minute demo script:** scan sweep → robot waters only the two dry pots → harvests one basil stem and drops it into a kitchen box → dashboard compares water and transport against delivered produce (state assumptions).\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** cutting tool unsafe or unavailable → pluck whole pots instead of stems · perception of real plants is fiddly → colour-tag pots · impact numbers are estimates → label them as such.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* moderate · *Technical depth* moderate · *Impact* clear resource story · *Leukerbad* strong (alpine supply chain, wellness cuisine).\n\n### Concept 4 — Roaming resource butler: mobile collection + sorting (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Collection is the expensive part of recycling in a pedestrian resort. A mobile robot collects from rooms, corridors or spa areas and hands over to the sorting cell — or sorts on board.\n- **Hardware:** **Helios** if on site (two AR-class arms, omni base, on-board Jetson). Otherwise: another vendor's quadruped or wheeled base (Unitree Go2, Genisom, AGIBOT D1) carrying a basket, docking to the fixed ROKAE cell from Concept 1.\n- **Architecture / stack:** Navigation on the base vendor's stack (or Helios ROS 2) → docking marker (AprilTag) at the cell → ROKAE arm unloads the basket and runs the Concept 1 sorter · task dispatch via a simple web app; optional SKYLANDX scan for the venue map.\n- **48-hour plan:** H0–6 two sub-teams: base navigation / docking and arm sorting · H6–24 each works standalone · H24–40 integrate at the docking point · H40–48 end-to-end runs; cut scope to \"base arrives, arm unloads 3 items\" if unstable.\n- **3-minute demo script:** tap \"collect room 12\" → base fetches a basket → docks → arm unloads and sorts → dashboard shows trips saved.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** two vendors' stacks double the integration risk → keep the interface to one message (\"docked\") · Helios access unlikely → never plan on it before seeing it · fallback is Concept 1 alone, which still stands as a complete demo.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* — system-level, multi-robot. *Technical depth* — high if it works live. *Impact* — collection logistics. *Leukerbad* — car-light village, steep streets (indoor demo only).\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROKAE arm + **D-Robotics RDK X5** | Low-power on-edge perception; strong \"watts per sorted item\" story | Low–medium (camera + inference on RDK, results over network to the SDK host) |\n| ROKAE arm + **WUJI Hand** | Dexterous handling of irregular waste or delicate disassembly | Medium–high (mounting, power, separate hand SDK) |\n| ROKAE arm + **Unitree / Genisom / AGIBOT quadruped** | Mobile collection feeding a fixed sorting cell | Medium (only a docking handshake between stacks) |\n| ROKAE arm + **SKYLANDX MetaCam** | Fast 3D model of the workspace or venue for planning and the pitch | Low (offline scan) |\n| ROKAE arm + **Galbot / Galaxea / Spirit AI models** | Learned grasping instead of heuristics | High — model portability to a ROKAE arm is ⚠️ unverified; ask first |\n| ROKAE arm + **OriginFlow EMG** or **XREAL** | Teleoperated demonstration capture or operator overlay for exception handling | Medium–high |\n| ROKAE **HSA joints** + **coprod** / **Leadshine** | Custom low-cost mechanism (for example a bin-lid opener) | High for 48 hours; only with vendor help |\n\n## Questions to ask the ROKAE Robotics engineers on site\n\n1. Which exact models are here (SR3 / SR5 / CR7 / AR5 / Helios), how many, and do they come with a gripper, a camera and a mounting plate?\n2. Which xCore SDK version matches the controllers on site, and can we get the package and examples offline right now?\n3. From **Python**, can we read tool force / joint torque and set Cartesian stiffness, or is that C++ real-time only? What is the real-time rate?\n4. Is there a working ROS 2 package for these units — which distro, with MoveIt config and URDF?\n5. How do we drive a gripper: controller digital I/O, tool-flange I/O / RS485, or our own USB device?\n6. What are the safe speed and force limits you want us to keep with people around the table, and how do we recover from a protective stop without you?\n7. Can you share measured power draw (idle vs. moving) for the arm, or lend a power meter?\n8. For the CR35 parcel-sorting and fruit-picking cases: which vision and grasp planning was used, and is any of it reusable here?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick ROKAE Robotics for T03 if** your idea is a fixed-cell, contact-aware manipulation task (sorting, disassembly, produce handling) and you can supply perception, a gripper and compute yourself — and you confirmed in hour one that the SDK exposes what you need.\n- **Avoid if** your idea needs mobility, outdoor or snow operation, environmental sensing, or a ready-made AI model — choose a quadruped, mobile-manipulator or VLA vendor and use ROKAE at most as the arm.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots product page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — xMate CR product page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/545/xMateCR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html)\n- [ROKAE case — CR35 warehouse picking and sorting](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2629/CR35-Advances-Large-Scale-Warehouse-Picking-&-Sorting.html)\n- [ROKAE case — embodied intelligence in smart warehousing (JD Logistics, AR5)](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/2630/Embodied-Intelligence-in-Action:-Advancing-the-Future-of-Smart-Warehousin.html)\n- [ROKAE — Commercial Services cases (fruit picking)](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/208/Commercial-Services.html)\n- [ROKAE — General Industry cases (battery, sorting, catalyst)](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/343/General-Industry.html)\n- [ROKAE SDK documentation index](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [ROKAE ROS 2 package manual](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/ROS2)\n- [ROKAE Robotics company profile (this vault)](../ROKAE.md)\n","ROKAE-T04-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T04\nchallenge_title: \"Elderly Care & Rehabilitation\"\nfit: 4\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, brief]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation — Brief\n\n**Fit: 4/5** — Best pick if your idea involves a robot arm *touching or being held by a person* (rehab exercise, massage, tele-examination); weak if you need mobility, companionship or a ready-made AI model.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **xMate SR3 / SR5** (3 / 5 kg, Cartesian stiffness adjustable **0–3000 N/m**, 48 V, ~14–17 kg) or **AR5** 7-DoF arm (joint torque sensors, **10 kg** arm). What is actually on site is unconfirmed — check first |\n| Strongest proof point | Integrator applications on ROKAE arms: **AI massage robot** (SR series), moxibustion therapy robot, remote ultrasound diagnosis, ward auscultation and ultrasound robot — all undated case pages on rokae.com, none a certified ROKAE medical device |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | **Assist-as-needed rehab arm**: therapist drag-teaches an exercise path, the arm replays it in low-stiffness impedance mode while the patient holds a handle; a screen shows range of motion and effort |\n| Biggest risk | Hardware availability plus setup: 1 kHz real-time impedance needs a **Linux real-time kernel and a direct cable** to the controller. Human contact also needs strict force and speed limits |\n| Pairs well with | **OriginFlow** (EMG effort / intent), **XREAL** (AR therapy guidance, remote therapist view), **D-Robotics** RDK (pose estimation at the edge), **WUJI** hand (handover), a mobile base from **Unitree / AGIBOT** |\n\n## Why\n- Force control is the core product: joint torque sensing, drag teaching, joint and Cartesian impedance, direct torque — all exposed in the SDK.\n- Open developer path: official GitHub org with **C++ / Python SDK** and a **ROS 2 Humble + MoveIt 2** stack (URDF, Gazebo), Apache-2.0.\n- A track record of contact-with-body applications (massage, ultrasound, moxibustion) makes the pitch credible.\n- Leukerbad angle is direct: thermal spa plus rehab clinics equals massage, physiotherapy and therapist shortage.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Mobility, fall detection, night rounds or delivery — unless a Helios (160 kg wheeled dual-arm) is on site.\n- Companionship or cognitive-stimulation ideas — no speech, VLA or agent stack is public.\n- Teams without a Linux PC, camera and end-effector of their own.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html) · [AR humanoid arm](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html) · [Healthcare cases](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/206/Healthcare.html)\n- [xCore SDK introduction](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/SDK_Intro) · [ROKAE GitHub org](https://github.com/RokaeRobot) · [rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T04-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/T04-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","ROKAE-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, products]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T04 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](ROKAE-T04-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Read this first:** ROKAE sells force-controlled arms, not care robots. Its value for T04 is a compliant arm with an open SDK that is safe enough to build a contact demo on. Which units are in Leukerbad is **not confirmed** — verify at the vendor table before committing.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)\n\n![xMate SR series (SR3, SR5)](../assets/rokae-xmate-sr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** first choice for anything that touches or is held by a person — rehab exercise partner, massage / physio stroke, tele-palpation, feeding or handover aid. Light enough to clamp to a table. This is the arm ROKAE itself lists for its \"AI Massage Robot\" application.\n\n| Spec | SR3 | SR5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** |\n| Reach | **705 mm** | **919 mm** |\n| Arm weight | ~13.8 kg | ~16.5 kg |\n| Max tool speed | ≤1.5 m/s | ≤2.0 m/s |\n| Cartesian stiffness range | **0–3000 N/m** | **0–3000 N/m** |\n| Rotational stiffness range | 0–300 Nm/rad | 0–300 Nm/rad |\n| Force control relative accuracy | 0.5 N, 0.1 Nm | 0.1 N, 0.02 Nm |\n| Repeatability | ±0.03 mm | ±0.03 mm |\n| Power / protection | 48 VDC · IP54 | 48 VDC · IP54 |\n| Controller | MSC box, ~1.2 kg, IP20, 5 DI / 5 DO, external e-stop input | same |\n| Safety paperwork | \"Function Safety\" certificate listed (standard not named on page) | ISO 15066 certificate file listed (covers SR5, CR7, CR12, CR20) |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · **Price:** not public · **On site:** likely but unconfirmed.\n- **Developer interface:** xCore SDK (C++ / Python / C#), ROS 2 Humble + MoveIt 2 config for SR3 / SR4 / SR5. Non-real-time: joint and Cartesian moves, force-control commands, drag teaching and path playback, collision-detection settings, I/O. Real-time (up to **1 kHz**): joint position, Cartesian position, joint impedance, Cartesian impedance, direct torque.\n\n### AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)\n\n![AR series (AR3, AR5, AR10)](../assets/rokae-ar.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** 7-DoF, human-arm-like kinematics with torque sensors in every joint. The redundancy lets you keep the elbow away from the patient while the hand follows a path. Best option for a dual-arm or \"arm on someone else's mobile base\" build.\n\n| Spec | AR3 | AR5 | AR10 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DoF | 7 | 7 | 7 |\n| Payload | **3 kg** | **5 kg** | **10 kg** |\n| Reach | 650 mm | 683 mm | 815 mm |\n| Arm weight | **8 kg** | **10 kg** | 19.5 kg |\n| Joint torque sensors | standard | standard | standard |\n| Force control resolution (reference) | ≤0.05 N, ≤0.02 Nm | same | same |\n| Tool speed | ≥2 m/s | ≥2 m/s | ≥2 m/s |\n| Bus / power | EtherCAT 1 ms · 48 VDC | same | same |\n| Protection / noise | IP54 · ≤60 dB | same | same |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping (10,000th unit delivered Oct 2026) · **Price:** not public · **On site:** plausible, unconfirmed.\n- **Developer interface:** dedicated SDK branch **0.7.x-AR**; the ROS 2 stack ships MoveIt configs for **AR5L / AR5R** (left and right arm). The C++ real-time docs have an AR-specific section — ask what differs. No stiffness range is published for AR.\n- **Caution:** ≥2 m/s tool speed is far above what is acceptable next to a frail person. Cap speed in software.\n\n### Helios (Helios II)\n\n![Helios (Helios II)](../assets/rokae-helios.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** the only ROKAE platform that moves and manipulates — fetch-and-carry, handover, medication trolley, caregiver logistics. Built for factories, not homes.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Arms | Dual, **5 kg per arm** (AR-class force-controlled arms) |\n| Base | Omnidirectional, **1.5 m/s** |\n| Height / weight | 1493–2000 mm · **160 kg** |\n| Runtime | ~4 h |\n| Sensors | 2× 3D LiDAR, head stereo camera |\n| Compute | Jetson Orin NX / AGX Orin / Thor options |\n| DoF | 23 in spec table (intro text says 42 — page is inconsistent) |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ deployed in industry (five units at Schaeffler Taicang, Oct 2026) · **Price:** not public · **On site:** possible, unconfirmed.\n- **Developer interface:** ROS 2 + Python SDK listed; gamepad control. Navigation stack details not public. Specs above are taken from the company profile.\n- **Caution:** 160 kg near elderly people is a safety argument you must address in the pitch (geofence, low speed, keep-out distance).\n\n### xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)\n\n![xMate CR series (CR5, CR7, CR12, CR18, CR20, CR35)](../assets/rokae-xmate-cr.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** fallback if no SR is available. CR7 / CR12 have the reach for a full-back massage path or a bedside task, but they are heavier arms; keep stiffness and speed low.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Payload range | **5–45 kg** across the family |\n| Reach range | **988–2246 mm** |\n| Sensing | Joint torque sensing for collision detection |\n| Safety paperwork | ISO 15066 certificate file listed for CR7, CR12, CR20 |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping · **Price:** not public.\n- **Developer interface:** same xCore SDK; ROS 2 MoveIt configs for CR7 / CR12 / CR18 / CR20 / CR35.\n\n### xMate ER Pro (ER3 Pro / ER7 Pro)\n\n![xMate ER Pro (ER3 Pro / ER7 Pro)](../assets/rokae-xmate-er-pro.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** the 7-DoF arm used in ROKAE's remote-ultrasound case (ER7 Pro: 7 kg, 850 mm). ⚠️ unverified status — legacy line, not on the current English product pages. The ROS 2 stack still has configs named ER3, ER7, Pro3, Pro7. Use only if one happens to be on site.\n\n### xCore / iNex\n\n![xCore / iNex](../assets/rokae-xcore.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** xCore is the controller every arm above runs; the SDK talks to it. Requires **xCore v3.2.1 or later** for the current GitHub SDK. iNex (embodied-AI control system) is 🧪 — named in a press release, no public developer access.\n\n**Not relevant for T04:** NB / XB industrial robots (not collaborative), CMR mobile manipulators (factory AMRs, 150–1500 kg chassis), welding package, Human.X (🧪, availability unclear), HSA joints (only if you plan to build a custom rehab device — not realistic in 48 hours).\n\n## What ROKAE Robotics already does in this domain\n\nNothing elderly-care specific. What exists is a set of **medical and wellness applications built by integrators on ROKAE arms**. All case pages are undated, name no partner, and none is a certified ROKAE medical device.\n\n| Application | Arm | Type | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AI massage robot | SR series | Wellness — force-controlled contact with the body | [SR product page](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html) |\n| Moxibustion therapy robot | xMate + vision | Wellness / traditional therapy | [ROKAE Japan case](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1196/%E3%81%8A%E7%81%B8%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html) (via profile) |\n| Remote ultrasonic diagnosis robot | ER7 Pro per Japanese case page | Tele-examination under teleoperation | [EN case](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/1028/Remote-Ultrasonic-Diagnosis-Robot.html) |\n| Ward auscultation and ultrasound robot | cobot, model not named | Bedside examination | [EN case](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/1030/Ward-Auscultation-&-Ultrasound-Robot.html) |\n| Total knee arthroplasty assisted robot | cobot, model not named | Surgery assistance | [EN case](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/995/Total-Knee-Arthroplasty-Assisted-Robot.html) |\n| Tumor biopsy navigation and positioning robot | cobot, model not named | Surgery positioning | [EN case](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/show/1029/Tumor-Biopsy-Navigation-and-Positioning-Robot.html) |\n\n- The healthcare case index has filter categories for **\"Rehabilitation\" and \"Nursing\" but shows no cases under them** — an honest signal that rehab and care are intended markets without a published reference.\n- No pilot in a care home, rehab clinic or home setting was found. No European or Swiss healthcare deployment was found.\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **No announced elderly-care or rehabilitation product or roadmap.** Everything below is inference.\n- *Inference:* ROKAE positions itself as a force-control component supplier (10,000 AR arms, 80+ partners integrating them). Care robots from other makers may end up using ROKAE arms or HSA joints; ROKAE itself is unlikely to ship a care robot soon.\n- *Inference:* Helios and Human.X are aimed at factories (Schaeffler deployment). A service or care variant has not been announced.\n- iNex 🧪 and a VLA \"intelligent core\" 🔭 are named in marketing; no developer access, no care-specific skills.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What you get | T04 use | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP) | Apache-2.0. Ubuntu 18.04 / 20.04 / 22.04 (x86_64, aarch64), Windows 10/11. Non-real-time motion, force-control commands, drag and path playback, collision detection, I/O. Real-time up to **1 kHz**: joint / Cartesian position, joint / Cartesian impedance, direct torque | Assist-as-needed rehab, constant-force massage, tele-operation | ✅ |\n| [xCoreSDK-Python](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python) | Apache-2.0. Python **3.8–3.12**, binaries from GitHub Releases (v0.7.1), `base_example.py` | Fast scripting with vision and LLM code. ⚠️ unverified whether real-time impedance is exposed in Python | ✅ |\n| [rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2) | Apache-2.0, v0.0.4. **ROS 2 Humble / Ubuntu 22.04**, MoveIt 2 configs for 14 models (incl. SR3 / SR5, CR7 / CR12, AR5L / AR5R), ros2_control hardware plugin, URDF + meshes, Gazebo helpers | Plan and simulate before you get arm time; collision-aware motion around a person model | ✅ |\n| [SDK docs](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK) | Quick start, API reference, real-time troubleshooting (network, torque control, AR models), FAQ | Setup and debugging | ✅ |\n| Pendant drag teaching | Hand-guide and record a path without code | Therapist \"teaches\" an exercise or massage stroke in seconds | ✅ |\n| Pretrained models, datasets, teleoperation toolkit | — | — | not public |\n\n**Real-time mode prerequisites (from the SDK introduction):** PC wired directly to the controller's control LAN port (no switch), Linux real-time kernel, CPU governor set to performance, total loop under 1 ms. Realtek network cards are recommended over Intel. Budget half a day for this, or stay in non-real-time mode.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **Hardware on site is unknown.** The whole plan depends on an SR, CR or AR arm being available to your team.\n- **Not a medical device.** No CE-MDR or similar clearance is claimed for any ROKAE arm or application. Pitch as a research prototype.\n- **Human contact safety is your job.** An ISO 15066 certificate file is listed for some models, but your tool, path and force limits are untested. Use a phantom or a healthy volunteer's forearm, low stiffness, capped speed, e-stop in hand.\n- **No perception, speech or AI stack included.** Cobots have no GPU and no camera — bring a PC, depth camera and your own models.\n- **No gripper or therapy end-effector confirmed.** Plan to 3D-print or tape a handle or soft roller.\n- **No mobility** unless Helios is on site; no fall detection, companionship or medication-management features.\n- **Thin English support content** for care use cases; case pages give no technical detail to copy from.\n- **Web search quota was exhausted during this research** — findings rely on official ROKAE pages, docs and GitHub fetched directly; third-party 2026 news on ROKAE in elder care could not be searched.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE Robotics company profile (internal)](../ROKAE.md)\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html) (specs via company profile)\n- [ROKAE — Healthcare case index](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/206/Healthcare.html)\n- [ROKAE — official site (EN)](https://www.rokae.com/en/)\n- [ROKAE docs — SDK index](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [ROKAE docs — xCore SDK introduction](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/SDK_Intro)\n- [GitHub — ROKAE Robotics org](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [GitHub — xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — xCoreSDK-Python](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python)\n- [GitHub — rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n- [ROKAE Japan — moxibustion therapy robot case](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1196/%E3%81%8A%E7%81%B8%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html) (via company profile)\n- [ROKAE Japan — teleoperated ultrasound robot case (ER7 Pro)](https://www.rokae.com/jp/case/show/1189/%E9%81%A0%E9%9A%94%E6%93%8D%E4%BD%9C%E5%9E%8B%E8%B6%85%E9%9F%B3%E6%B3%A2%E8%A8%BA%E6%96%AD%E3%83%BB%E6%B2%BB%E7%99%82%E3%83%AD%E3%83%9C%E3%83%83%E3%83%88.html) (via company profile)\n- [TechNode Global (PR Newswire) — 10,000th AR arm, Oct 2026](https://technode.global/?p=996114) (via company profile)\n","ROKAE-T04-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"ROKAE Robotics\"\nslug: ROKAE\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, playbook]\n---\n# ROKAE Robotics × T04 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [ROKAE Robotics profile](../ROKAE.md) · [Brief](ROKAE-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](ROKAE-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Before anything else (first 30 minutes):** confirm which ROKAE arm your team can use, its xCore version (SDK needs **v3.2.1+**), whether a tool flange adapter or gripper is supplied, and where the e-stop is.\n\n**Safety rules for every concept below:** research prototype, not a medical device · demo on a phantom, mannequin or a healthy volunteer's forearm — never neck, head or spine · low stiffness, capped speed and force · one team member holds the e-stop whenever a person is in reach.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Assist-as-needed rehab exercise arm (safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Upper-limb rehab after stroke, fracture or shoulder surgery needs hundreds of guided repetitions; therapists are scarce. A compliant arm lets one therapist set up an exercise once and the patient repeat it with measured assistance or resistance.\n- **Hardware:** xMate SR3 / SR5 (or AR5) clamped to a table, 3D-printed handle on the flange, laptop. Optional: OriginFlow EMG band for effort, XREAL glasses or a tablet for the patient view.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - *Perception:* robot's own joint and flange state (position, force) — no camera needed for the core loop. Optional webcam + MediaPipe pose for compensation detection (shoulder shrug, trunk lean).\n  - *Planning:* therapist records the exercise by **drag teaching**; store the path; per repetition compute lag behind the path and adapt assistance.\n  - *Control:* non-real-time path playback plus force-control commands for the first version; upgrade to **real-time Cartesian impedance** (stiffness scheduled along the path, range 0–3000 N/m on SR) if the real-time kernel is ready.\n  - *Compute:* laptop, Ubuntu 22.04, xCoreSDK C++ or Python; simple web dashboard (range of motion, repetitions, peak force, session trend).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - *H0–6:* connect SDK, read state, jog, record and replay a dragged path. Print the handle.\n  - *H6–24:* compliance along the path, three modes (passive guide / assist / resist), per-repetition metrics, speed and force caps, soft workspace limits.\n  - *H24–40:* dashboard, therapist \"prescription\" screen, optional EMG or pose module, optional LLM session summary for the therapist.\n  - *H40–48:* rehearse with three different users, record a backup video, freeze the code.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) one line on the therapist shortage in rehab resorts → (0:30) \"therapist\" drags the arm through a shoulder-flexion arc, 10 seconds → (1:00) \"patient\" holds the handle, arm guides gently; patient goes limp and assistance rises; patient pushes and the arm yields → (2:00) dashboard shows range of motion and effort per repetition, plus a generated session note → (2:30) someone pushes the arm off-path to show it stays soft; close with clinic rollout story.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** real-time kernel not ready → stay on non-real-time force commands and drag/playback (still a full demo). No SDK access to force readings → estimate effort from path lag. No arm time → develop against the Gazebo model from rokae_ros2.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* — therapist-programmable by demonstration, no code. *Technical depth* — variable impedance, quantified outcomes. *Impact* — multiplies one therapist across several patients. *Leukerbad* — rehab clinics and elderly spa guests are the village's core business.\n\n### Concept 2 — Force-limited massage / physio stroke with body tracking\n\n- **Problem → value:** Massage and manual therapy are labour-intensive and physically hard on therapists. A robot that applies a repeatable, pressure-limited stroke can take over routine warm-up or relaxation passes.\n- **Hardware:** SR5 or CR7 (reach), soft silicone roller or ball tool, depth camera (RealSense-class) on a tripod, massage phantom or volunteer forearm / calf. Optional: D-Robotics RDK X5 for on-edge pose estimation.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - *Perception:* depth camera → body-segment point cloud → surface normals along a chosen muscle line; hand-eye calibration once.\n  - *Planning:* generate a surface-following stroke; pressure level chosen by the user (voice or tablet), hard-capped in code.\n  - *Control:* hybrid — position along the surface, **constant force along the normal** (force-control commands or Cartesian impedance with a force offset). Stop instantly on force spike or motion of the limb.\n  - *Compute:* laptop with GPU for perception; SDK over Ethernet. MoveIt 2 (ROS 2 Humble) for approach and retreat.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - *H0–6:* SDK up, tool mounted, constant-force press on a foam block working.\n  - *H6–24:* camera calibration, surface path from point cloud, stroke on phantom, safety monitors.\n  - *H24–40:* pressure levels, voice or tablet control, \"too much\" stop word, limb-moved re-planning.\n  - *H40–48:* tune, rehearse, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** problem (0:30) → camera scans forearm, path is drawn on screen (0:45) → robot performs three strokes at \"light\", then \"medium\"; live force plot stays inside the band (1:15) → volunteer moves the arm, robot lifts and re-plans (0:30) → business case for spas and clinics.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** calibration eats time → skip vision and drag-teach the stroke on the phantom, keep force control. Unstable contact → lower stiffness, slower stroke, softer tool. Judges worried about safety → run on phantom only and show the force log.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* — moderate (ROKAE already lists an AI massage robot application; your edge is perception plus safety logic). *Technical depth* — hybrid force/position on a deformable, moving surface. *Impact* — therapist workload. *Leukerbad* — a thermal-spa resort is the textbook customer.\n\n### Concept 3 — Tele-care touch: remote therapist's hands in a mountain village\n\n- **Problem → value:** Specialists are hours away from alpine villages, and elderly patients travel badly in winter. A force-reflecting remote arm lets a physiotherapist or doctor examine, palpate or guide a movement from elsewhere.\n- **Hardware:** SR5 or AR5 as the patient-side arm; operator side = a second ROKAE arm in drag mode (if two are available) or a 6-DoF input (SpaceMouse, VR / XREAL controller, phone IMU). Camera at the patient side. Optional: OriginFlow EMG to map operator grip to contact pressure.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - *Perception:* patient-side video stream plus flange force stream to the operator.\n  - *Planning:* none — direct teleoperation with workspace clamp, velocity scaling and force ceiling.\n  - *Control:* real-time **Cartesian impedance** following the operator pose at low stiffness, so contact force stays bounded even if the operator overshoots. Force is shown to the operator as a visual bar, sound, or (two-arm setup) reflected through the leader arm.\n  - *Compute:* two laptops linked over the venue network (WebRTC video, small UDP or WebSocket control channel); real-time loop stays local to the patient-side PC.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - *H0–6:* SDK up on patient arm, pose streaming from the input device, clamp limits.\n  - *H6–24:* stable impedance following, force display, video link, latency measurement.\n  - *H24–40:* watchdog (freeze softly on link loss), session recording, optional AR overlay in XREAL glasses, optional second arm as leader.\n  - *H40–48:* run across two rooms, rehearse, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** map showing Leukerbad to the nearest hospital (0:20) → \"therapist\" in another room sees video and force bar, guides the arm to palpate a phantom knee and to lead a patient's hand through a movement (1:30) → pull the network cable: arm goes soft and holds (0:30) → roadmap: tele-ultrasound, which ROKAE arms already do in integrator projects (0:40).\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** real-time kernel needed for good feel → fall back to low-rate non-real-time pose targets at reduced speed. Network jitter → same-room demo with an artificial screen wall. Only one arm → SpaceMouse or phone as input.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* — bilateral feel with safety by compliance. *Technical depth* — real-time control plus networking and failure handling. *Impact* — access to care in remote regions. *Leukerbad* — one road in, winter closures, elderly guests.\n\n### Concept 4 — Mobile care assistant with compliant handover (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Caregivers spend much of a shift fetching and carrying. A mobile manipulator that brings water, medication boxes or a walking aid and hands them over gently frees time for actual care.\n- **Hardware:** Helios if on site; otherwise a ROKAE AR5 / SR3 mounted on or parked beside another vendor's mobile base (Unitree, AGIBOT, Galaxea — check payload and 48 V power). Gripper or WUJI hand, RGB-D camera, Jetson or laptop. Optional: SKYLANDX scan for the room map.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - *Perception:* open-vocabulary detector for the object and the person's hand; speech-to-text for requests.\n  - *Planning:* LLM task planner calling a small skill library (navigate, pick, approach person, handover); MoveIt 2 for arm motion.\n  - *Control:* position control for pick; **impedance mode for the handover** — release when the person pulls (force threshold), yield if bumped.\n  - *Compute:* Jetson AGX Orin on Helios or a laptop; models local or cloud depending on venue network.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - *H0–6:* confirm platform, power and mounting; SDK and base both drivable from one PC.\n  - *H6–24:* pick from a fixed shelf, navigate between two waypoints, force-triggered release.\n  - *H24–40:* voice request, person and hand detection, medication-reminder scenario, error recovery.\n  - *H40–48:* cut scope to what works three times in a row; backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** \"Bring my pills\" (0:15) → robot drives to shelf, picks box (1:00) → approaches seated person at low speed, offers the box, releases on a gentle pull (0:45) → person bumps the arm, it yields (0:20) → caregiver time-saved claim and next steps (0:40).\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** highest integration risk (two vendors, power, mounting, calibration). Fallback 1: static arm at a \"care station\" doing pick and compliant handover only. Fallback 2: base delivers, human loads. Helios weight (160 kg) near people → low speed and a marked keep-out zone.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* — force-aware handover in an LLM-planned task. *Technical depth* — full perception-to-control chain. *Impact* — caregiver relief. *Leukerbad* — clinics, hotels and care residences with staff shortages.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROKAE arm + **OriginFlow** EMG | Measure patient effort or intent; adapt assistance; show muscle activation next to robot force | Low–medium (independent data stream, fuse on the laptop) |\n| ROKAE arm + **XREAL** glasses | AR exercise guidance for the patient or remote view for the therapist | Medium (separate Android XR app, share state over WebSocket) |\n| ROKAE arm + **D-Robotics** RDK X5 / S100 | Pose estimation and body tracking at the edge, no GPU laptop needed | Low–medium (ROS 2 on both sides; check Humble compatibility) |\n| ROKAE arm + **WUJI** Hand | Dexterous grasp and gentle handover of small objects (pill box, cup) | Medium–high (flange adapter, power, second SDK) |\n| ROKAE arm + **Unitree / AGIBOT / Galaxea** mobile base | Mobile fetch-and-carry when no Helios is available | High (mounting, 48 V supply, calibration) |\n| ROKAE arm + **SKYLANDX** MetaCam | Fast 3D map of the room or care station for planning | Medium (offline scan, export to planner) |\n| ROKAE arm + **Binabik AI** / **Spirit AI** / **Galbot** models | Agent or VLA layer on top of a force-controlled arm | High and ⚠️ unverified — depends on whether their stack supports third-party arms |\n| ROKAE HSA joints + **Leadshine** / **coprod** parts | Custom rehab device (for example a one-joint elbow trainer) | High — not advised for 48 hours |\n\n## Questions to ask the ROKAE Robotics engineers on site\n\n1. Which arms are here (SR3 / SR5 / CR / AR5), how many, and what xCore version do they run (SDK needs v3.2.1+)?\n2. Is real-time mode (1 kHz joint / Cartesian impedance, direct torque) licensed and enabled on these units, and does the **Python** SDK expose it or only C++?\n3. Do you have a PC with a real-time kernel already set up that we can borrow, or an image we can flash?\n4. How do we read external force / joint torque estimates from the SDK, and at what rate?\n5. What are the software limits we can set for human contact — maximum TCP speed, force, power, collision-detection sensitivity — and do they hold in real-time mode?\n6. What changes for AR arms (SDK 0.7.x-AR): stiffness range, redundancy / elbow control, dual-arm coordination?\n7. Is a gripper, flange adapter or force-controlled tool supplied, and is tool-payload identification automatic?\n8. Can you share details of the AI massage or remote-ultrasound integrations (control mode used, force levels, safety concept) and whether ROKAE has any rehab or nursing project in progress?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick ROKAE Robotics for T04 if** your idea is a contact-rich arm task (rehab exercise, massage / physio, tele-examination, compliant handover), an SR / CR / AR arm is confirmed on site, and someone on the team is comfortable with Linux and C++ or Python.\n- **Avoid if** you need mobility, fall detection, companionship or a ready-made VLA / agent stack, if no ROKAE arm is free for your team, or if nobody can own the safety limits for human contact.\n\n## Sources\n- [ROKAE Robotics company profile (internal)](../ROKAE.md)\n- [ROKAE — SR cobots](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/349/SR-Cobots.html)\n- [ROKAE — Humanoid Force-Controlled Arm AR](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/595/Humanoid-Force-Controlled-Arm-AR.html)\n- [ROKAE — Wheeled Dual-Arm Robot Helios](https://www.rokae.com/en/product/show/596/Wheeled-Dual-Arm-Robot-Helios.html) (specs via company profile)\n- [ROKAE — Healthcare case index](https://www.rokae.com/en/case/206/Healthcare.html)\n- [ROKAE docs — xCore SDK introduction](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK/SDK_Intro)\n- [ROKAE docs — SDK index](https://docs.rokae.com/en/docs/SDK)\n- [GitHub — ROKAE Robotics org](https://github.com/RokaeRobot)\n- [GitHub — xCoreSDK-CPP](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-CPP)\n- [GitHub — xCoreSDK-Python](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/xCoreSDK-Python)\n- [GitHub — rokae_ros2](https://github.com/RokaeRobot/rokae_ros2)\n"}});
